'' =============================================================================
''
''   File....... test-vo-j-035-usb-wxpin-on-running-pair.spin2
''   Purpose.... VO-J-035 on SILICON: on a RUNNING USB smart-pin pair (%11011, DIR high), does a WXPIN -- re-issued with the same
''               word, or changed full-speed <-> low-speed -- take effect without a reset, so that the baud the WXPIN set is in force and
''               the NEXT packet is transmitted and received intact?  (The baud is what is proved; the FS/LS bit D[14] is not observable
''               on this self-sensing pair -- see WHAT IS DECIDED.)
''   Authors.... Iron Sheep Productions, LLC
''   E-mail..... stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... Oct 2026
''   Updated.... Oct 2026
''
'' =============================================================================

{ WHAT IS DECIDED  (register VO-J-035; study S-395; task 409)

  The P2 Documentation says of WXPIN on the lower pin of a USB pair (silicon-doc-text.txt:4581): "Once established, these settings can
  be changed on-the-fly without resetting the USB smart pins. This is necessary when talking to both 'full-speed' and 'low-speed'
  devices over a USB hub."  D[15] = 1 host, D[14] = 1 full-speed / 0 low-speed, D[13:0] = (rate FRAC clkfreq) >> 16 (:4583-:4587).
  The earlier test test-vo-f372-usb-wrpin-on-running-pair.spin2 (result EF-093, P2-EMPIRICAL-FINDINGS.md:1681-1698) re-issued WXPIN on
  the running pair AFTER its live WRPIN changes, as a step nobody judged; the next packet then came back empty (n = 0) in 5 of 5
  (EF-093 "Characterisation, not asked", :1695-1697).  Nobody knows whether that was the pin or the packet routine, because that
  routine writes a value only while the upper pin's IN is high (F372 packet routine: testp #PIN_DP), and a WXPIN/WYPIN/WRPIN/RDPIN/AKPIN
  is an acknowledge that lowers IN until the next event (silicon-doc-text.txt:3845-:3849; for the USB pair :4589: "You must do an AKPIN
  on the upper pin to return its IN pin to a low state, in order to detect the next FIFO-not-full signal").  A third candidate came
  from this program's RUN 1 (see RUN HISTORY): F372's characterisation packet also started from IDLE -- it followed the previous
  packet's automatic EOP ("SE0, SE0, J, then IDLE", :4603-:4604) and a WXPIN, with no J written -- while every F372 packet that arrived
  started from a driven, settled J (WYPIN #3, SETTLE_CLK 4,000 clocks, F372 engine).
  THIS PROGRAM SEPARATES THE CANDIDATES, AND PROVES THE NEW SETTING IS IN FORCE:
    * every arm drives J and lets it settle BEFORE its WXPIN site (J DRIVE below), and the line state (bits 2..0) is judged: J before
      the WXPIN site in every arm (gate JS), and J in every control's baseline just before its packet (gate JB) -- a miss is a RIG
      FAIL where the J was driven at full speed, and takes the low-speed half (and D2) out of the verdict where it was driven at low
      speed (gates JL, JD);
    * its packet routine does NOT read any IN flag.  Value k is written at a FIXED deadline measured from the SOP write (below);
    * after every WXPIN (and in the control arms, where there is no WXPIN) it re-establishes a clean baseline before the packet:
      settle, AKPIN on both pins (silicon-doc-text.txt:3722, :3847-:3849), RQPIN of the status word -- and the baseline is JUDGED:
      SOP-in (bit 4) and EOP-in (bit 5) must be clear, or the arm's round is not GOOD;
    * the receiver shares the baud generator with the transmitter (:4614 "Only the baud generator is common"), so a pair that IGNORED
      a WXPIN would still decode its own output.  The new BAUD is therefore proved two ways: (a) the MEASURED DURATION of the packet
      (first byte received -> fourth byte received, three byte periods = 24 bit periods) must read the baud the WXPIN selected, and
      (b) the mismatch control X shows a pair that is NOT at the baud of the schedule returns a packet that is not intact.

  SCOPE OF THE SPEED PROOF  (review finding 1)
    The WXPIN word has three fields: D[15] host/device, D[14] full-speed/low-speed signalling, D[13:0] baud (:4583-:4587).  What this
    test proves is the BAUD (D[13:0]): the duration of the packet, the schedule that X shows is wrong at the other baud, and the
    self-sensing decode are all functions of the baud generator only.  The baud differs between the FS and LS words (3932 vs 491)
    and so does D[14], but the packet cannot tell which of the two changed.  EVERY reading below therefore says "the baud set by the
    WXPIN is in force and the next packet is intact" -- it never says "FS/LS mode changed".
    D[14] (idle polarity: FS idle = DM low / DP high, LS idle = DP low / DM high, :4585) has no documented, judgeable observable on this pair, and no
    judged observation for it was built, for these reasons: (1) the only observer is the pair's own receiver, which reads the pair's own
    output (:4614) in the same speed convention as the driver -- its status line-state bits 0..3 (J in, K in, SE0 in, steady state,
    :4626-:4629) report J / K relative to the speed in force, so a pair that applied D[14] to driver and receiver and a pair that
    ignored it both read their own J as J and their own SOP (KJKJKJKK, :4604) as an SOP; (2) the Silicon Doc gives no status-bit
    polarity per speed to compare against, only idle line levels, and the pad levels cannot be read back: the lower pin's IN is the
    smart pin's own event output (:4591), not the pad; (3) any independent observer (another pin, a scope, an attached device) is
    outside this rig (P0/P1 only, bare board).  A claim about D[14] needs a different rig (a device or an analyser on the lines).

  RUN HISTORY
    RUN 1 (2026-10-09, log tests/logs/debug_261009-140053.log; .bin 24,942 bytes, byte-identical to a build of the source committed at
      439771aa) = NO VERDICT.  Gates K and N1 failed (RIG FAIL); gates L and S failed too.  Every arm in every round: n = 0, ev 0 (no
      status-bit 4..15 change at all, so no SOP-in), baseline $0C and last status $0C (bit 3 steady + bit 2 SE0; r1 CFS read $4C for
      both, the error bit 6 also set).  Gates P, Q, O held (lateness 21..24 clocks in every arm that wrote data, 0 in N2; stl short arms
      111, long arms 20,109..20,115, D2 20,113, N2 20,073).
      N2 and X "passed", but only vacuously: with no SOP-in in any arm, an empty N2 and a not-intact X say nothing about the decoder.
    DIAGNOSIS (not proven -- it is what this version's controls test): every arm wrote its SOP from host IDLE, which with nothing
      attached is SE0 (host IDLE = two 15k pull-downs, silicon-doc-text.txt:4583 / :4599; the baselines read steady SE0).  The F372
      program on the same P0/P1 rig drove J (WYPIN #3) and settled 4,000 clocks before every packet, and its packets arrived (EF-093).
      Run 1 cannot show whether the transmitter drove the line at all: the event mask $FFF0 hid the line-state bits 0..3.
    WHAT CHANGED for run 2 (R1-R4 of task 409, then review fixes F1-F3):
      R1  every arm drives J before its WXPIN site: bounded wait for the DP IN (:4595, :4608), WYPIN #3 = J (:4602), J_SETTLE_CLK =
          4,000 clocks (>= 7 bit periods at either speed, :4624-:4626).  It runs before the GETCT at the WXPIN site, so no arm's stl and
          no control-vs-WXPIN-arm difference changed.  D2 drives J again before its own WXPIN site (D1's packet ended in the automatic
          EOP and IDLE).  Gate JS judges the J before the site.
      R2  the baseline line state is printed for every arm and JUDGED for the controls (gate JB); the WXPIN arms print it only (J DRIVE).
      R3  every sample of the packet window contributes its line state to "seen" (A_SEEN plus the logged events); the event log, its
          mask and LOGMAX are unchanged (gate O); the pass lengths and the lateness worst case are re-derived in PACING.
      R4  the VERIFIED OPCODES block was re-read from the pnut-ts -d -l 1.55.8 listing of this version.
      F1  (review) the DP-IN wait is NOT a gate: nothing shows that DP IN rises after a fresh DIRH on an empty FIFO (F372 wrote its
          first WYPINs after a fresh start without testing DP IN, F372 engine control_arm; its IN-gated packet routine ran only after a
          J write and a 4,000-clock settle).  The timeouts are counted and printed; the J is written either way and judged by JS.
      F2  (review) the judged line state is bits 2..0 (J in, K in, SE0 in) = %001; bit 3 (steady) is printed only.  A J miss where the J
          was driven at LOW speed is not a RIG FAIL: the low-speed convention of the status bits is not observed anywhere yet, so such a
          miss takes the low-speed half (gate JL) and, for D2, D2 itself (gate JD) out of the verdict, as gate L does.
      F3  (review) the per-pass line-change COUNT (3 instructions per pass) became one OR per plain pass, which restores run 1's
          event pass (36 clocks) and most of the event-vs-deadline margin (PACING).

  J DRIVE AND LINE STATE  (task 409 R1-R3, review fixes F1-F3)
    Each arm: usb_start (the documented start, :4593) ends with drive_j: clear seen_; wait for the DP IN (the output FIFO has room --
    "wait for the upper pin's IN to be high", :4595 / :4608), bounded by JW_TIMEOUT_CLK = 10,000 clocks; WYPIN #3,#PAIR (J, "like IDLE,
    but driven", :4602) -- written whether or not the wait ran out; wait J_SETTLE_CLK = 4,000 clocks; RQPIN the status (A_JST).  Then
    the GETCT at the WXPIN site.  D2 runs drive_j again on D1's pair.
    J_SETTLE_CLK vs 7 bit periods: FS 117 clocks, LS 934 clocks (control W recomputes both and fails the run if 4,000 is short); 7 bit
    periods of J clear SOP-in and EOP-in (:4624-:4625), which the baseline needs clear.
    DP-IN WAIT -- AN OBSERVATION, NOT A GATE.  A_JW = the clocks until DP IN was seen high, or NO_TIME when the 10,000-clock bound ran
      out ("jw timeout" on the raw line).  The gates section prints "DP IN not high within 10000 clocks before the J write: k of 75"
      (15 arms x 5 rounds, D2 included, counted alike: the Documentation makes no distinction between a fresh start and a drained
      FIFO).  The J that follows is judged by JS either way, so a timeout can neither fail the run nor make a reading.
    LINE STATE = status bits 2..0: [2] SE0 in, [1] K in, [0] J in (silicon-doc-text.txt:4627-:4629); "J" = %001.  Bit 3, steady state
      (:4626), is printed beside it ("steady 0/1") and never judged: whether a driven, unchanging J sets it is not observed yet.
    WHERE A J MISS GOES (gates JS and JB) -- by the speed at which that J was driven:
      J driven at FULL speed  (CFS A1 B D1 N1 N2 X SCF SB; JB for CFS SCF N1 N2 X): RIG FAIL.  F372 (EF-093) read its driven J as
                              J (%001) at full speed on this rig, so a full-speed J that does not read J is a broken rig.
                              Readings reachable: NO VERDICT only.
      J driven at LOW speed   (CLS A2 C SCL SC; JB for CLS SCL): gate JL fails -- the low-speed arms A2 B C D1 SB SC are NOT
                              JUDGED (exactly as when gate L fails), each with its printed cause line; A1 and (gate JD holding) D2
                              stay judged.  No run of ours has read a low-speed J, so a miss there is not proof of a broken rig.
                              Readings reachable: DISTURBED (a judged full-speed arm NONE), INCONCLUSIVE; never SURVIVES.
      D2's J (driven on D1's pair, at low speed, after D1's WXPIN): a miss fails gate JL AND gate JD -- the low-speed arms and D2 are
                              NOT JUDGED; A1 alone stays judged.  The verdict line names D2 as not judged.
                              Readings reachable: DISTURBED (A1 NONE), INCONCLUSIVE; never SURVIVES.
      The J step is judged in EVERY arm, so no judged arm's packet follows a J step that failed.  N2 and X are non-vacuous: their empty
      / not-intact records come from a line that read J (gate JB).
    BASELINE OF THE WXPIN ARMS (A1 A2 B C D1 D2 SB SC) -- PRINTED ONLY, NOT JUDGED.  Expected from the Documentation: the last-set state
      (J) remains (:4610), J is "like IDLE, but driven" (:4602), and the IDLE levels follow D[14] (:4585), so a pair that applied the
      WXPIN drives J in the convention of the speed then in force; its receiver reads its own output (:4614) with J / K relative to the
      same speed, so the expected baseline line state is J (%001); in SB / SC -- read about 30 clocks after the WXPIN by instruction
      count (WXPIN, CALL sched, 3 MOV, RET, CALL pkt4s, MOV, JMP, AKPIN, then the RQPIN), less than 7 bit periods at FS (117) and at LS
      (934) -- the steady bit may be clear.  It is not judged because the Documentation does not say that a held line state follows a
      WXPIN at once (that is part of the "on-the-fly" claim under test, :4581), and because a J read here cannot tell the FS/LS bit
      D[14] (SCOPE).  A non-J baseline in a WXPIN arm is therefore a raw observation about that WXPIN, read beside the arm's packet
      result -- never a rig failure (gate JS judged the J before the WXPIN) and never a verdict on its own.  The per-arm count line
      prints, for every arm, in how many rounds the baseline was J, marked "judged" (controls) or "printed only".
    LINE STATES SEEN (R3, F3): the engine ORs every sample it does NOT log into seen_ (one OR instruction on the plain path only);
      every sample it DOES log is a status word in the event log, and cog 0 ORs those in (line_seen).  So "seen SE0/K/J %bbb" on each
      raw line is the OR of the line state of every sample of the window -- complete unless the log overflowed (gate O fails the run
      then).  K seen = the transmitter drove the line (the SOP is KJKJKJKK, :4604; nothing else drives K on a bare board); %001 = the
      line stayed J.  It is a sampled record: a state shorter than the gap around it (18 clocks on a plain pass, 42 across an event
      pass, 64 across a write; an FS bit is 16.7) can be missed.  A packet holds K for many bit periods (the SOP's closing KK, then
      the NRZI runs inside the bytes), so a transmitted packet is expected to show K; "K not seen" alone is not proof that nothing
      was driven.  Printed, not judged.

  SETTLE  (review finding 2)
    The first version waited WX_CLK = 20,000 clocks (100 us) after every WXPIN, which would hide an effect that is only immediate.
    Every arm now takes a GETCT at the "WXPIN site" (just before the WXPIN, or where it would be) and the engine records the clocks from
    there to the SOP write (A_STL, printed as stl, held in a band by gate Q).  Two settles are run:
      LONG  (CFS CLS A1 A2 B C D1 D2 N1 N2 X)   WXPIN, call sched (14 clocks), WX_CLK wait 20,000 clocks, AKPIN + baseline, packet:
                                                 stl = 20,000 + about 110-120 clocks (gate Q band 20,000..20,300).
      SHORT (SCF SCL SB SC)                      WXPIN, call sched (14 clocks), AKPIN + baseline, packet, NO wait: stl = the code path
                                                 only, about 110-120 clocks by instruction count (2 clocks per ALU instruction, 4 per
                                                 CALL / RET / JMP, 3..10 for the one hub write; gate Q band 0..300).  That path is the
                                                 minimum the engine needs: the baseline (AKPIN, RQPIN, record write) is judged and the
                                                 packet routine must start; about 6.6 FS bit periods, 0.8 LS bit period.
    Run 1 measured stl = 111 (short arms) and 20,109..20,115 (long arms; D2 20,113, N2 20,073: N2 writes nothing, so its stl ends
    at the start of the routine).  This version adds 2 clocks before the SOP write (the OR in the first poll pass: 6 instructions
    before its POLLCT1, not 5) and nothing else between the GETCT and the SOP write (the packet setup is run 1's, instruction for
    instruction; seen_ is cleared in drive_j and the J drive runs before the GETCT): expected about 113 / 20,113, N2 unchanged.
    SB / SC are the short-settle versions of B / C.  SCF (start FS) and SCL (start LS) are their controls: the same instructions in
    the same order, and the same times, as SB / SC, minus only the WXPIN itself (2 clocks); they must be GOOD (gate S) or SB and SC are
    not judged.  The long arms' controls are CFS / CLS the same way.  The settle used by each arm is printed per arm (stl), in the
    per-arm count line (min..max), and in every VERDICT line.

  RIG  (BARE BOARD -- NOTHING ATTACHED)
    One USB pair: P0 = DM (even, lower) and P1 = DP (odd).  "The receiver receives not just input from another host/device, but all
    local output, as well" (silicon-doc-text.txt:4614), so the pair reads its own output (the same self-sense as F372, which carried
    $A5 $5A $3C $C3 in every round, EF-093).  P0 and P1 are DRIVEN by the program (host); attach NOTHING to them: they carry no external
    load.  No other pin is used.

  PACING  (BLOCKER 1 of the adversarial review: the old routine wrote value k on the first poll pass after a gap, and a pass took 50-70
    clocks, so at full speed a data byte could land after the shifter emptied -> automatic EOP -> a false short packet whose occurrence
    depended on the baud phase.  Replaced by deadlines.)
    * Value 0 (SOP) is written at once; its write instant T0 is taken with GETCT immediately before the WYPIN.  Value k (k = 1..4) is
      DUE at T0 + G0 + (k-1) * STEP, i.e. at (8k - 4) bit periods after T0: G0 = 4 bit periods, STEP = 8 bit periods (a byte is 8 bit
      periods on the wire; SOP = KJKJKJKK, 8 bits, :4604).  FS bit = 65536 / 3932 = 16.667 clocks: G0 = 67, STEP = 133.  LS bit = 65536 / 491
      = 133.476 clocks: G0 = 534, STEP = 1068 (each = the bit-period count rounded to the nearest clock; control W recomputes them).
    * One cog does both jobs.  ADDCT1 sets CT1 to the next deadline; the loop samples the status of DM (RQPIN) every 18 clocks on a
      plain pass, logs every change of status bits 4..15 with the CT, ORs every sample it does not log into seen_ (A_SEEN), and
      POLLCT1 tells it the deadline has passed.  A second cog was not used: the single loop already samples every 18 clocks (byte
      period at full speed = 133 clocks), and one cog keeps the write instant and the receive instants on one clock with no hand-off.
      Pass lengths (re-derived for run 2 from the listing; run 1's loop was the same minus the OR):
        .poll  RQPIN st_ ; MOV tg_,st_ ; AND tg_,mask_ ; CMP tg_,prevm_ WZ ; IF_NZ JMP #.event ; OR seen_,st_ ;
        .chkt  POLLCT1 WC ; IF_NC JMP #.poll
        plain pass = seven 2-clock instructions + the taken JMP 4 = 18 clocks (run 1: 16); RQPIN to the start of POLLCT1 = 12 (run 1: 10).
        event pass, RQPIN to the start of POLLCT1 = RQPIN, MOV, AND, CMP 8 + taken JMP 4 + handler (GETCT, MOV, CMP, six conditional
               instructions, IF_NC ADD = ten 2-clock instructions) 20 + JMP #.chkt 4 = 36 (run 1: 36 -- the OR is not on this path;
               the logged status word carries the sample's line state, which cog 0 ORs in).
      The GETCT that takes the write instant executes 12 clocks after the start of the POLLCT1 that saw the deadline pass (POLLCT1 2,
      not-taken JMP 2, CMP 2, not-taken JMP 2, ALTS 2, MOV 2; unchanged).  Worst case: the deadline passes just after a POLLCT1 and the
      next pass logs an event: 6 + 36 + 12 = 54 clocks, +2 to the WYPIN = 56 (run 1: the same).  On plain passes only: 6 + 12 + 12 =
      30 clocks (run 1: 28), inside LATE_MARGIN = 48.  Run 1 measured lateness 21..24 clocks with 16-clock passes and no events; about
      2 more is expected here.  The 54 needs a logged event in the pass that straddles a deadline; the events the schedule produces
      (SOP-in, then each received byte) are EXPECTED (from the byte timing, not measured) about 4 bit periods (66 FS clocks) from the
      nearest deadline, farther than the 18 + 36 = 54 clocks of a plain pass and the event pass after it, so the worst case is not
      expected; the margin is 66 - 54 = 12 clocks (run 1: 66 - 52 = 14).  If a receiver event does land that close and a write is later
      than LATE_MARGIN, gate P fails the run (NO VERDICT) -- it can never produce a reading.  Line-state changes do NOT take the event
      path (the mask $FFF0 is unchanged), so the frequent line transitions inside a byte never lengthen a pass.
    * Each write's instant (GETCT taken 2 clocks before the WYPIN) is stored relative to T0 (A_W1..A_W4, printed as "w a/b/c/d") with the
      largest lateness (instant - deadline, A_LATE).  GATE P: a lateness above LATE_MARGIN = 48 clocks in ANY arm = RIG FAIL (not only
      in the controls: a late write makes the arm uninterpretable).  Derivation of the 48 (review finding 3): value k is written into
      the second FIFO level while value k-1 is in the shifter; value k-1 ends at (8k + d) bit periods after T0 (d in 0..1 = the wait for
      the next bit boundary), value k is due at (8k - 4), so the write may arrive at most 4 bit periods = 66.67 FS clocks late before
      the shifter empties and the pin sends an automatic EOP (:4604).  The write reaches the pin after the lateness measured (GETCT
      instant - deadline) by: the 2-clock GETCT-to-WYPIN gap (WYPIN_CLK) plus the WYPIN-to-smart-pin latency, which is NOT measured
      here.  The part of that latency that is constant is common to the SOP write and the data writes (every deadline is measured from
      the SOP write instant) and cancels; only its jitter counts, and it is covered by an ASSUMED allowance PIN_LAT_ALLOW = 12 clocks
      (an assumption, stated, not measured).  48 + 2 + 12 = 62 < 66 (control W checks it against 66.7 rounded down; the rounded G0_FS
      is 67).  The old margin of 60 left 66.7 - 60 - 2 = 4.7 clocks for that latency.  The other way: value k-1 enters the shifter at
      (8(k-1) + d) <= (8k - 7) bit periods, before value k is written at (8k - 4), so the holding level is empty and the two-level FIFO
      (:4589) never overflows.  At low speed the same margin is 0.36 bit period.
    * The observation window is OBS_BITS = 80 bit periods from the start of the routine (FS 1333, LS 10678 clocks); the last write is due
      at 28, its byte ends at about 40-41 bit periods and the EOP about 3 later: W checks 80 > 28 + 8 + 3 + 16 (= 55, conservative).  Every wait is bounded by CT1 events; the packet
      routine ends at the window end whatever the status says.

  DURATION  (BLOCKER 2b)
    The first byte received (byte-toggle change after SOP-in) to the fourth byte received is exactly three byte periods = 24 bit periods:
    the four bytes $A5 $5A $3C $C3 contain no run of six 1-bits, so no bit stuffing changes the period (A5 = 1010_0101, 5A = 0101_1010,
    3C = 0011_1100, C3 = 1100_0011, LSB first; the longest run is the 1111 of 3C).  FS: 24 x 16.667 = 400 clocks (1,572,864 / 3932 =
    400.0).  LS: 24 x 133.476 = 3,203 clocks (1,572,864 / 491 = 3203.4).  Tolerance +/- 25 % (DUR_TOL_PCT): FS 300..500, LS 2402..4003.
    Why 25 %: each of tb1 and tb4 is late by at most the sampling gap its toggle fell in, so dur is off by at most the larger gap
    (re-derived for run 2): 18 clocks on a plain pass, 42 across an event pass (36 + POLLCT1 2 + JMP 4), 64 across a data write (the
    pass to POLLCT1 12, POLLCT1 to the WYPIN 16, the bookkeeping after it 36: TJZ, MOV, SUB, CMPS, MOV, ADD, JMP #.wr, then MOV, SUB,
    ALTD, MOV, ADD, CMP, ADDCT1, ADDCT1, JMP #.poll); plus the receiver's one-bit resolution (17 FS clocks): <= 64 + 17 = 81 clocks of
    400 at full speed (run 1 stated 71 from a 54-clock gap), inside the 100 of the 25 %; a few percent at low speed.  (An event pass
    followed by a write is the deadline-straddling case of PACING, watched by gate P.)  The two windows are 4.8x apart, so a wrong
    baud can never fall inside (control W checks the windows are disjoint).  A GOOD arm needs the bytes AND its duration inside the window of the baud it is supposed to be at.

  ARMS  (each arm = a fresh documented start of the pair, START_CLK 20,000 clocks, GETCT at the WXPIN site, the arm's WXPIN step if
         any, then settle + AKPIN + RQPIN baseline (settle: LONG = 20,000-clock wait, SHORT = none), then ONE packet: SOP, $A5, $5A,
         $3C, $C3.  The fresh start = DIRL, WRPIN drive, WXPIN <start word> on DM, DIRH, wait -- the documented start,
         silicon-doc-text.txt:4593 -- then the J drive (bounded DP-IN wait, WYPIN #3, J_SETTLE_CLK 4,000, RQPIN; J DRIVE above).
         FS = host full-speed word, LS = host low-speed word.)
         Timing of every arm, clocks (all arms: START_CLK 20,000 after DIRH, the J drive, then GETCT; stl = GETCT to SOP write):
           arm        start  WXPIN at the site   schedule  settle   stl expected
           CFS        FS     none                FS        long     20,000 + path (path = about 110-120; run 1 measured 111, this version ~113)
           CLS        LS     none                LS        long     20,000 + path
           A1         FS     FS (same)           FS        long     20,000 + path + 2
           A2         LS     LS (same)           LS        long     20,000 + path + 2
           B          FS     LS                  LS        long     20,000 + path + 2
           C          LS     FS                  FS        long     20,000 + path + 2
           D1         FS     LS                  LS        long     20,000 + path + 2
           D2         (D1's pair, still running) FS FS     long     20,000 + path + 2   (D1's SETTLE_CLK 4,000 and D2's own J drive precede the GETCT)
           N1, N2     FS     none                FS        long     20,000 + path (N1 sends SOP + 2 bytes, N2 nothing)
           X          FS     none                LS        long     20,000 + path
           SCF        FS     none                FS        short    path (control of SB, SC)
           SCL        LS     none                LS        short    path (control of SB, SC)
           SB         FS     LS                  LS        short    path + 2
           SC         LS     FS                  FS        short    path + 2
         The controls differ from their WXPIN arms only by the WXPIN instruction (2 clocks); gate Q holds every arm in its band.
    CFS  control, positive   start FS, no WXPIN step, packet at FS schedule.   Must be GOOD (FS) every round.        (gate K)
    CLS  control, positive   start LS, no WXPIN step, packet at LS schedule.   Must be GOOD (LS) every round.        (gate L)
    A1   re-issue, same      start FS, WXPIN FS (same word) on the running pair, packet at FS.       GOOD (FS baud)?
    A2   re-issue, same      start LS, WXPIN LS (same word) on the running pair, packet at LS.       GOOD (LS baud)?
    B    change FS -> LS     start FS, WXPIN LS on the running pair, packet at LS.                   GOOD (LS baud)?
    C    change LS -> FS     start LS, WXPIN FS on the running pair, packet at FS.                   GOOD (FS baud)?
    D1   change, middle      start FS, WXPIN LS, packet at LS  (the low-speed step in the middle of D, now judged).  GOOD (LS baud)?
    D2   change back         the same pair, still running: J again, WXPIN FS, packet at FS.         GOOD (FS baud)?
    N1   negative control    start FS, no WXPIN step, only SOP, $A5, $5A are sent.   Must return exactly n = 2: $A5 $5A.  (gate N1)
    N2   negative control    start FS, no WXPIN step, NOTHING is sent.               Must return exactly n = 0, no byte.  (gate N2)
    X    negative control    start FS, no WXPIN step, the packet written at the LS SCHEDULE.  Must NOT be intact.        (gate X)
    SCF  control, positive   start FS, no WXPIN step, packet at FS, SHORT settle.      Must be GOOD (FS) every round.      (gate S)
    SCL  control, positive   start LS, no WXPIN step, packet at LS, SHORT settle.      Must be GOOD (LS) every round.      (gate S)
    SB   B, short settle     start FS, WXPIN LS, the packet after the code path only (no wait), at LS.   GOOD (LS baud)?
    SC   C, short settle     start LS, WXPIN FS, the packet after the code path only (no wait), at FS.   GOOD (FS baud)?
  N1 and N2 are certain by construction: a transmitter that is given two data bytes cannot put four on the wire, and one that is given
  none cannot put any.  They prove the decoder reports "fewer than four / none arrived" as fewer than four / none.
  X is certain to be not intact, from the documentation alone: the SOP occupies the full-speed transmitter for 8 bit periods (:4604); with
  the FIFO then empty the pin sends the automatic EOP (:4604) and EOP-in rises about 10-11 FS bit periods (about 180 clocks) after the SOP
  write; the first data byte is written 4 LS bit periods (534 clocks) after the SOP write, 3x later.  The decoder counts bytes only
  between SOP-in and the first EOP-in and ignores everything after it, so whatever the pin does with the late bytes cannot make it
  four bytes in order.  Its outcome therefore does not rest on any undocumented pin behaviour.  If X comes back intact the speed is not
  what the rig thinks: RIG FAIL.  X is the proof that a pair NOT at the baud of its schedule returns a non-intact packet.
  WHAT AN IGNORED WXPIN LOOKS LIKE, per arm (review finding 4):
    B, D1, SB  (an FS pair, the WXPIN to LS ignored, given the LS schedule): the same situation as X, so it reads like X -- n < 4
               with an early EOP-in, not GOOD, counted NONE (or MIXED).
    C, D2, SC  (an LS pair, the WXPIN to FS ignored, given the FS schedule): this is the mirror of X and is NOT a control, because the
               pair is then given five values inside 467 clocks while the LS SOP alone takes 1068 clocks, and what a WYPIN does when the
               two-level FIFO is full is undocumented (:4608 only says to wait for the upper pin's IN).  The outcome is therefore NOT
               pre-registered beyond this: such an arm cannot be GOOD (not four bytes in order AND a duration in the FS window), so it is
               NONE / MIXED by count or duration; if a WYPIN stalls instead, the next write is late and gate P fails the run (NO VERDICT).
    A1, A2     (the same word re-issued): an ignored re-issue cannot be told from a taken one; they prove only that re-issuing does not
               disturb the packet.
  Every raw line prints dwin, which window (FS / LS / none) the measured duration fell in, and each NONE
  arm's count line says in how many rounds the duration was in the OTHER baud's window.
  The low-speed direction is carried by the DURATION: arms CLS, A2, B, D1, SCL, SB must measure LS (2402..4003) and CFS, A1, C, D2, SCF,
  SC FS (300..500).

  CONTROLS (a failure of W, JS / JB on a full-speed-start arm, P, Q, O, K, N1, N2 or X prints RIG FAIL and NO VERDICT;
            L, JL, JD and S take arms out of the verdict instead; the DP-IN wait is printed, never judged)
    W  : the pair is even/odd and in the free set; the drive word decodes to its fields and equals its Spin2 symbol composition; both
         WXPIN words decode (D[15] = 1 host, D[14] = 1 FS / 0 LS, D[13:0] = the FRAC computation done at run time, two MSBs 0); the
         schedule constants (G0, STEP, DUR, OBS) of both speeds equal the bit-period counts computed at run time; LATE_MARGIN plus the
         GETCT-to-WYPIN gap plus PIN_LAT_ALLOW (48 + 2 + 12 = 62) is below the 4-bit-period under-run bound (66.7; checked against 66);
         the two duration windows are disjoint; the window covers the packet; J_SETTLE_CLK (4,000) is at least 7 bit periods at FS
         (117) and at LS (934), computed at run time.
    (DP-IN wait: NOT a gate.  Timeouts are counted, "k of 75", and marked on the raw lines; see J DRIVE AND LINE STATE.)
    JS : in every FULL-SPEED-START arm (CFS A1 B D1 N1 N2 X SCF SB) and round the status after the J drive, before the WXPIN site, has
         line state J (A_JST bits 2..0 = %001; bit 3 not judged).
    JB : in every full-speed-start CONTROL arm (CFS SCF N1 N2 X) and round the baseline just before the packet has line state J (A_BASE
         bits 2..0 = %001).  Run 1's baselines ($0C: bits 2..0 = %100, SE0) fail this gate on their own lines.
    (J DRIVE AND LINE STATE above gives where a miss of a LOW-speed J goes: gates JL and JD below.)
    P  : in EVERY arm and round the largest write lateness <= LATE_MARGIN = 48 clocks.
    Q  : in EVERY arm and round the settle (stl) is in its band: the short arms SCF SCL SB SC 0..300 clocks, every other arm 20,000..20,300.
         (This proves the short arms were short and the long ones long, so the settle the verdict line states is the one that ran.)
    O  : no arm's event log overflowed (LOGMAX = 24 events kept per arm; a GOOD packet logs about 8).
    K  : arm CFS GOOD in every round.
    N1 : arm N1 returned exactly n = 2 with bytes $A5 $5A and the other two slots unfilled, every round (clean baseline too).
    N2 : arm N2 returned exactly n = 0 with all four slots unfilled, every round (clean baseline too).
    X  : arm X NOT intact (clean baseline, and not [n = 4 with the four bytes in order]) in every round.
         (N1, N2 and X are also held to a J baseline by gate JB, so an empty or not-intact record cannot come from a line that was
         never driven -- run 1's N2 and X "passed" that way.)
    L  : arm CLS GOOD in every round.  If it does not hold, the low-speed arms (A2, B, C, D1, SB, SC) are NOT JUDGED (they are printed
         raw), the verdict line says so, and a SURVIVES cannot be printed.  (Not a RIG FAIL: the full-speed half is still judged.)
    JL : every J driven at LOW speed reads J (bits 2..0 = %001) in every round: JS of CLS A2 C SCL SC and D2, JB of CLS and SCL.  If it
         does not hold, the low-speed arms (A2, B, C, D1, SB, SC) are NOT JUDGED, exactly as for gate L, with its own cause line, and a
         SURVIVES cannot be printed.  (Not a RIG FAIL: the low-speed status convention has not been observed on any run.)
    JD : D2's J (driven on D1's pair after D1's WXPIN to LS) reads J before D2's WXPIN site in every round.  If it does not hold, D2 is
         NOT JUDGED (and JL fails with it), the verdict line names D2 as not judged, and a SURVIVES cannot be printed.
    S  : arms SCF and SCL (short settle, no WXPIN) GOOD in every round.  If it does not hold, SB and SC are NOT JUDGED, the verdict line
         says so, and a SURVIVES cannot be printed.  (Not a RIG FAIL: the long-settle arms are still judged.)
    T  : the measuring cog reports done within RIG_TIMEOUT_SEC = 5 s (clkfreq x 5 = 1.0e9, below 2^31).

  DECODING  (done by cog 0 from the engine's event log; every step below is exercised by a judged arm)
    The engine logs (status word, CT) at every change of status bits 4..15 (SOP-in, EOP-in, error, byte toggle, last byte).  Cog 0 walks
    the log in order: SOP-in rising before any EOP-in opens the packet and fixes the toggle reference; while the packet is open and EOP-in
    has not been seen, each change of the toggle bit is one received byte (its value = bits 15:8); the first EOP-in rising closes the
    packet for good (bytes after it are never counted).  n = bytes counted (may exceed 4); tb1 / tb4 = CT of the first / fourth byte;
    dur = tb4 - tb1.  The EOP-in time and the error bit (:4623) are PRINTED but are not part of GOOD (EOP-in is used only to close the
    packet).  Other printed, unjudged diagnostics: SOP-in time, last status word, per-write times, event count, the DP-IN wait (jw),
    the line states seen in the window (seen: A_SEEN ORed with every logged event's status word), the steady bit (bit 3) of jst and
    base, the baseline line state of the WXPIN arms, and -- for an arm that missed its required record -- its whole event list.
    (jst bits 2..0 are judged by gates JS / JL / JD, the controls' baseline bits 2..0 by gates JB / JL.)

  PRE-REGISTERED READINGS (fixed before the run; printed by the program).  GOOD round of a packet arm = clean baseline (bits 4 and 5
  clear) AND n = 4 with $A5 $5A $3C $C3 in order AND dur inside the window of the arm's expected baud.  Each judged arm is classed
  ALL (GOOD in all 5 rounds), NONE (in none) or MIXED.  Judged arms are A1 always; D2 when gate JD holds; A2, B, C, D1 when gates L
  and JL hold; SB and SC when gates L, JL and S hold.  Every VERDICT line carries the settle of every arm (long arms and short arms,
  min..max clocks, measured); a verdict with arms not judged prints a "NOT JUDGED in this verdict:" line naming them and the gate.
  "The RIG-FAIL gates" below = W, JS, JB (full-speed start), P, Q, O, K, N1, N2, X.
    SURVIVES     : the RIG-FAIL gates hold, gates L, JL, JD and S hold (so EVERY WXPIN arm, D2 included, is judged), and every judged
                   arm is ALL -- WXPIN on a running pair (same word,
                   FS <-> LS, and back; after a 20,000-clock settle and after the short code-path settle) takes effect without a reset:
                   the baud set by the WXPIN is in force and the next packet is intact.  It does NOT say "FS/LS mode changed" (D[14] is
                   not observable here, see SCOPE OF THE SPEED PROOF).
    DISTURBED    : the RIG-FAIL gates hold (L, JL, JD, S may not -- then their arms are not judged and the verdict says so) and at
                   least one judged arm is NONE -- the packet after that WXPIN step was not GOOD in any round while its own no-WXPIN
                   control was (and the arm itself read J before its WXPIN, gate JS / JD).  Two causes, told apart by the raw record:
                   the WXPIN did not take effect (B, D1, SB then read like X: n < 4, an early EOP-in; C, D2, SC are not GOOD by count or
                   duration, behaviour beyond that undocumented :4608), or the pair was disturbed (the printed baseline line state after
                   the WXPIN, and seen, show whether the line was still J and whether the transmitter drove it).  The line names the
                   NONE arms and also the MIXED arms.  A SB / SC NONE with B / C ALL says the effect needs the settle.
    INCONCLUSIVE : the RIG-FAIL gates hold, no judged arm is NONE, and (a) some judged arm is MIXED, or (b) all judged arms are ALL but
                   gate L, JL, JD or S failed (the arms not judged are named).
    NO VERDICT   : a RIG-FAIL gate failed (the records are printed for diagnosis), or the measuring cog did not finish.  A DP-IN
                   timeout, a miss of a low-speed J, or a clear steady bit can never cause it on their own.
    The raw record of every arm in every round is printed (baseline status word and its line state, n, the four bytes, SOP-in /
    first-byte / fourth-byte / EOP-in times, duration, duration window, settle, lateness, write times, event count, last status word,
    DP-IN wait jw, status after the J drive jst and its line state, line states seen), so any result outside these readings is read
    from the log.

  DEBUGGER (EF-057): DEBUG_COGS = %0000_0001: the debug interrupt lives in cog 0 only.  Every pin operation runs in a dedicated PASM
    cog started by COGINIT; it writes results to a hub mailbox; cog 0 reports.  Every printed line is built in a hub buffer and
    sent by ONE debug() record.  Compile with pnut-ts -d; nothing on P62.

  CONFIGURATION WORDS  (WRPIN D = %AAAA_BBBB_FFF_MMMMMMMMMMMMM_TT_SSSSS_0, silicon-doc-text.txt:3731; %SSSSS :3807-:3841; USB :4579-:4593)
    drive  $0000_0076  TT=%01 SSSSS=%11011 bit0=0 = P_USB_PAIR | P_OE   (D data %1_11011_0, :4579)
    FS     X = $0000_CF5C  = host + full-speed + (12_000_000 FRAC 200_000_000) >> 16 = 3932 = $0F5C
    LS     X = $0000_81EB  = host + low-speed  + (1_500_000  FRAC 200_000_000) >> 16 = 491  = $01EB
    Pair addressing PAIR = DM | (1 << 6): S[10:6] = 1 additional pin (silicon-doc-text.txt:62 and :4424).
    WYPIN codes (:4597-:4605): 3 = output J (the J drive), $80 = SOP, then data bytes.  Status word (:4618-:4629): [5] EOP in, [4] SOP
    in, [7] byte toggle (cleared on SOP, toggled on each byte received), [15:8] last byte, [3] steady, [2] SE0 in, [1] K in, [0] J in.
    A byte is received when the toggle changes after SOP-in (:4632-:4637).

  VERIFIED OPCODES AND DATA  (re-read for run 2 from the pnut-ts -d -l v1.55.8 build 9/19/2026 listing of THIS version; hub offsets
    from the object start, cog addresses in brackets; condition IF_C = $C, IF_NC = $3, IF_NZ = $5 in bits 31:28; a relative CALL / JMP
    target = (cog address of the instruction + 1) + the 20-bit offset / 4)
    Symbol table (listing): X_FS = $0000_CF5C, X_LS = $0000_81EB, CFG_DRIVE = SYM_DRIVE = $0000_0076, G0_FS = $43 (67), STEP_FS = $85 (133),
      DUR_FS = $190 (400), OBS_FS = $535 (1333), G0_LS = $216 (534), STEP_LS = $42C (1068), DUR_LS = $C83 (3203), OBS_LS = $29B6 (10678),
      LATE_MARGIN = $30 (48), PIN_LAT_ALLOW = $C (12), WYPIN_CLK = 2, DUR_TOL_PCT = $19 (25), DUR_BITS = $18, OBS_BITS = $50,
      WX_CLK = START_CLK = $4E20 (20000), SHORT_MAX_CLK = $12C (300), SETTLE_CLK = J_SETTLE_CLK = $FA0 (4000), JW_TIMEOUT_CLK = $2710 (10000),
      STEADY_BITS = 7, LINE_MASK = 7 (%111), J_STATE = 1 (%001), BIT_STEADY = 8, J_PATH_FS / _LS / _D2 = 0 / 1 / 2, LOGMAX = $18,
      A_STL = 9, A_JW = $A, A_JST = $B, A_SEEN = $C, A_LOG = $D, ARMW = $3D (61), NARMS = $F, ROUNDW = $393 (915), MB_LONGS = $11E1
      (4577), DF = $B, LINE_MAX = $2BC (700), NO_TIME = $8000_0000, NO_BYTE = $FFFF_FFFF, BASE_MASK = $30, BIT_SOP = $10, BIT_EOP = $20,
      BIT_TOG = $80, ARM_D1 = 6, ARM_D2 = 7, ARM_X = $A, ARM_SCF = $B, ARM_SCL = $C, ARM_SB = $D, ARM_SC = $E.  CLKMODE $010009FB,
      CLKFREQ 200_000_000, XINFREQ 20_000_000.  DEBUG: 2 records, 24 bytes of DEBUG data.  Object (OBJ) 20,972 bytes, VAR 22,384
      bytes; the compiled binary 30,214 bytes.  (Run 1's build: OBJ 15,700, VAR 21,484, binary 24,942.)
    Engine at object offset $0C8; cog labels: usb_off [$062] usb_start [$067] drive_j [$070] .jw [$072] .jgo [$07C] .jdrv [$07E]
      sched_fs [$083] sched_ls [$087] pkt4s [$08B] pkt4 [$08D] pktgen [$08E] baseline [$090] packet [$096] .poll [$0A9] .chkt [$0AF]
      .first [$0BE] .wr [$0C1] .event [$0CA] .finish [$0D5] put [$0F7] cfg_drive_ [$0FA] x_fs_ [$0FB] x_ls_ [$0FC] .. mask_ [$103]
      txdata_ [$104]; registers mb_ [$109] respt_ [$10A] progp_ [$10B] rnd_ [$10C] roundno_ [$10D] xs_ [$10E] g0_ [$10F] step_ [$110]
      obs_ [$111] nvals_ [$112] st_ [$113] tg_ [$114] val_ [$115] txi_ [$116] prevm_ [$117] b_ [$118] t0_ [$119] tsop_ [$11A] dl_ [$11B]
      tend_ [$11C] tw_ [$11D] tl_ [$11E] ts_ [$11F] te_ [$120] late_ [$121] lgi_ [$122] novf_ [$123] w0_..w4_ [$124..$128]
      self_id_ [$129] twx_ [$12A] jt0_ [$12B] jw_ [$12C] jst_ [$12D] seen_ [$12E] logbuf_ [$12F..$15E].
    Instructions read back from the hub image (.lst hex dump):
      usb_start: wrpin cfg_drive_,#PAIR $FC05F440   wxpin xs_,#PIN_DM $FC161C00   dirh #PAIR $FD648041   waitx ##START_CLK $FF800027 $FD64401F
        call #drive_j $FDB00004 (at $280 [$06E] -> [$070])   ret $FD64002D
      drive_j: mov seen_,#0 $F6065C00   getct jt0_ $FD62561A   .jw: testp #PIN_DP wc $FD740240   if_c jmp #.jgo $CD900020
        getct jw_ $FD62581A   sub jw_,jt0_ $F182592B   cmp jw_,##JW_TIMEOUT_CLK wc $FF000013 $F2165910 ($13 << 9 | $110 = $2710)
        if_c jmp #.jw $CD9FFFE4   mov jw_,##NO_TIME $FF400000 $F6065800   jmp #.jdrv $FD900008   .jgo: getct jw_ $FD62581A
        sub jw_,jt0_ $F182592B   .jdrv: wypin #3,#PAIR $FC2C0640   waitx ##J_SETTLE_CLK $FF800007 $FD67401F ($7 << 9 | $1A0 = $FA0)
        rqpin jst_,#PIN_DM $FA865A00   ret
      D2's J: call #drive_j $FDB000F8 (at $18C [$031] -> [$070]), then getct twx_ $FD62541A, wxpin x_fs_,#PIN_DM $FC15F600.
      GETCT at the WXPIN site: getct twx_ $FD62541A   (the same word at every arm's WXPIN / non-WXPIN site, 15 arms, D2 included)
      wxpin x_fs_,#PIN_DM $FC15F600   wxpin x_ls_,#PIN_DM $FC15F800   akpin #PAIR $FC0C0240 (= wrpin #1,#PAIR, silicon-doc-text.txt:5488)
      baseline: akpin #PAIR $FC0C0240   rqpin st_,#PIN_DM $FA862600   mov val_,st_ $F6022B13
      packet: mov prevm_,st_ $F6022F13   and prevm_,mask_ $F5022F03   getct t0_ $FD62321A   cmp txi_,nvals_ wc $F2122D12
        if_c addct1 dl_,#0 $CA663600   if_nc addct1 tend_,#0 $3A663800
      .poll: rqpin st_,#PIN_DM $FA862600   mov tg_,st_ $F6022913   and tg_,mask_ $F5022903   cmp tg_,prevm_ wz $F20A2917
        if_nz jmp #.event $5D900070   or seen_,st_ $F5425D13 (OR, no flags: D = seen_ $12E, S = st_ $113)
      .chkt: pollct1 wc $FD700224   if_nc jmp #.poll $3D9FFFE0   cmp txi_,nvals_ wc $F2122D12   if_nc jmp #.finish $3D900088
      alts txi_,#txdata_ $F9962D04   mov b_,0-0 $F6023000   getct tw_ $FD623A1A   wypin b_,#PAIR $FC263040   tjz txi_,#.first $FB962C06
      mov tl_,tw_ $F6023D1D   sub tl_,dl_ $F1823D1B   cmps late_,tl_ wc $F252431E   if_c mov late_,tl_ $C602431E   add dl_,step_ $F1023710
      jmp #.wr $FD90000C   .wr: altd txi_,#w0_ $F98E2D24   add txi_,#1 $F1062C01   jmp #.poll $FD9FFF7C
      .event: getct te_ $FD62401A   mov prevm_,tg_ $F6022F14   cmp lgi_,#$30 wc $F2164430   if_c altd lgi_,#logbuf_ $C98E452F
        if_c mov 0-0,st_ $C6000113   if_c add lgi_,#1 $C1064401   if_c mov 0-0,te_ $C6000120   if_nc add novf_,#1 $31064601
        jmp #.chkt $FD9FFF68
      .finish: shr val_,#1 $F0462A01   A_STL: mov val_,tsop_ $F6022B1A   sub val_,twx_ $F1822B2A   A_JW: mov val_,jw_ $F6022B2C
        A_JST: mov val_,jst_ $F6022B2D   A_SEEN: mov val_,seen_ $F6022B2E (each then call #put)   setq #47 $FD645E28
        wrlong logbuf_,respt_ $FC625F0A   add respt_,#$C0 $F10614C0   put: wrlong val_,respt_ $FC622B0A
      mask_ = $0000_FFF0 (unchanged); pkt4s: mov nvals_,#5 $F6062405 then jmp #baseline $FD90000C ; pkt4: mov nvals_,#5 $F6062405 ;
      N1: mov nvals_,#3 $F6062403 ; N2: mov nvals_,#0 $F6062400 ; ret $FD64002D ; wrlong #MBOX_DONE,ptra as the last write.
    Semantics read from the P2KB (p2kb_get, not from memory): ADDCT1 D,S sets CT1 = D + S and writes D; the event fires once CT has
      PASSED CT1 (MSB of CT - CT1 = 0), a target already in the past fires at once; POLLCT1 copies the CT1 event flag to C and clears it;
      the flag is also cleared by ADDCT1.  RQPIN and WYPIN are 2 clocks each (WYPIN does not stall the cog).  TESTP {#}D WC sets C = IN
      of pin D (2 clocks).  OR D,S writes D = D | S, flags only with WC/WZ (2 clocks).
    The round body, read back in order (each arm: mov xs_, usb_start [which ends in drive_j], getct twx_, [wxpin], sched, packet call;
      the call targets were resolved from the relative call words against the label offsets above): CFS (xs_ = x_fs_, usb_start,
      getct twx_, sched_fs, pkt4), CLS (x_ls_, sched_ls, pkt4), A1 (x_fs_, wxpin x_fs_, sched_fs, pkt4), A2 (x_ls_, wxpin x_ls_,
      sched_ls, pkt4), B (x_fs_, wxpin x_ls_, sched_ls, pkt4), C (x_ls_, wxpin x_fs_, sched_fs, pkt4), D1 (x_fs_, wxpin x_ls_, sched_ls,
      pkt4) then D2 (call drive_j, getct twx_, wxpin x_fs_, sched_fs, pkt4), N1 (x_fs_, sched_fs, nvals_ #3, pktgen), N2 (x_fs_,
      sched_fs, nvals_ #0, pktgen), X (x_fs_, sched_ls, pkt4), SCF (x_fs_, sched_fs, pkt4s), SCL (x_ls_, sched_ls, pkt4s), SB (x_fs_,
      wxpin x_ls_, sched_ls, pkt4s), SC (x_ls_, wxpin x_fs_, sched_fs, pkt4s), then djnz rnd_ (one round = 15 arms, 15 getct twx_ sites,
      15 J drives: 14 usb_start + D2's drive_j).

  HOW TO RUN
    1. USB serial connected (DEBUG on P62/P63).  P0 and P1 carry no external load -- nothing attached to either.
       Compile: pnut-ts -d test-vo-j-035-usb-wxpin-on-running-pair.spin2
    2. Load to RAM with DEBUG enabled (pnut-term-ts, or PNut Ctrl-F10).
    3. SAVE THE WHOLE CONSOLE OUTPUT to a file and RECORD ITS FILE NAME (with the board and date) in the bench-round record -- it is the
       only record, and an EF entry cites it.
    4. Expected run time: well under a second (5 rounds x 15 arms; the J drives add about 16 x 4,000 clocks = 0.3 ms per round) and
       about 170 printed lines (run 1 printed 150 from its banner to DEBUG_END_SESSION; this version adds 20: eleven prediction lines,
       one control-W line, three legend lines, five gate lines; more if arms miss their record: each such arm also lists its event log,
       each JS / JB miss prints its own line, and a failed L / JL / JD / S prints its cause line and a NOT JUDGED line).  Done when you see one "VERDICT VO-J-035: ..." line (or "NO VERDICT"), then "=== END ===" and
       DEBUG_END_SESSION.  No END / DEBUG_END_SESSION line within 15 s = a hang or a lost session: power-cycle the board and send the
       LAST line you saw.
    5. Clock: _clkfreq = 200_000_000 is a compile-time constant that ASSUMES the board's 20 MHz crystal; the program cannot measure the
       crystal.  Control W checks the baud words and every schedule constant against that constant only (the run-time clkfreq is the
       compile-time value).  A board with a different crystal shows itself as broken or garbled DEBUG output (the debug link baud is
       derived from the same constant): do not use such a run.
    6. Pins: P0 (DM) and P1 (DP) are DRIVEN by the program as a USB host pair.  NOTHING may be attached to P0 or P1.  No other pin is touched.
}

CON ' ---- Rig Parameters ----
  _clkfreq          = 200_000_000
  DEBUG_COGS        = %0000_0001        ' EF-057: debugger in cog 0 ONLY

  PIN_DM            = 0                 ' lower (even) pin of the pair
  PIN_DP            = PIN_DM + 1        ' upper (odd) pin
  PAIR              = PIN_DM | (1 << 6) ' DM plus one additional pin (S[10:6] = 1)
  PIN_MAX_FREE_LOW  = 7                 ' free set: P0..P7 and P32..P47

  ROUNDS            = 5
  START_CLK         = 20_000            ' clocks after a start (DIRH) before the next step
  WX_CLK            = 20_000            ' LONG settle: clocks waited after a start / a WXPIN, before the baseline (100 us); the short-settle arms skip it
  SHORT_MAX_CLK     = 300               ' gate Q: a short-settle arm must reach its SOP write within this many clocks of the WXPIN site (code path ~110-120 by instruction count)
  SETTLE_CLK        = 4_000             ' clocks after a packet
  J_SETTLE_CLK      = 4_000             ' clocks the driven J settles before the WXPIN site (F372 settled 4,000 before its packets); >= 7 bit periods at either speed (control W)
  JW_TIMEOUT_CLK    = 10_000            ' bound of the wait for the DP IN before the J write (50 us = 75 LS bit periods); running out is printed, not judged
  STEADY_BITS       = 7                 ' 7 bit periods of J clear SOP-in / EOP-in (:4624-:4625) and set 'steady' (:4626) -- the J settle must cover them
  RIG_TIMEOUT_SEC   = 5
  MBOX_DONE         = 1
  NO_BYTE           = $FFFF_FFFF        ' a byte slot that never filled (a received byte is 0..255, never -1)
  NO_TIME           = $8000_0000        ' a time / duration that never filled (a real time of -1 clock is printed as -1, not as "none")

  RATE_FS           = 12_000_000        ' full-speed bit rate
  RATE_LS           = 1_500_000         ' low-speed bit rate

  X_HOST_FS         = $C000             ' D[15] = 1 host, D[14] = 1 full-speed
  X_HOST_LS         = $8000             ' D[15] = 1 host, D[14] = 0 low-speed
  BAUD_FS           = $0F5C             ' (12_000_000 FRAC 200_000_000) >> 16 = 3932
  BAUD_LS           = $01EB             ' (1_500_000 FRAC 200_000_000) >> 16 = 491
  X_FS              = X_HOST_FS | BAUD_FS
  X_LS              = X_HOST_LS | BAUD_LS

  ' Schedule, in bit periods of the speed the schedule is written for.  A clock count = (bits * 65536 + baud / 2) / baud.
  SCHED_G0_BITS     = 4                 ' SOP written -> first data byte written
  SCHED_STEP_BITS   = 8                 ' between data-byte writes (a byte is 8 bit periods on the wire)
  DUR_BITS          = 24                ' first byte received -> fourth byte received (three byte periods)
  OBS_BITS          = 80                ' observation window from the start of the packet routine
  G0_FS             = 67                ' = 4 x 16.667
  STEP_FS           = 133               ' = 8 x 16.667
  DUR_FS            = 400               ' = 24 x 16.667
  OBS_FS            = 1333              ' = 80 x 16.667
  G0_LS             = 534               ' = 4 x 133.476
  STEP_LS           = 1068              ' = 8 x 133.476
  DUR_LS            = 3203              ' = 24 x 133.476
  OBS_LS            = 10678             ' = 80 x 133.476
  LATE_MARGIN       = 48                ' clocks a write may be later than its deadline (gate P); 2.9 FS bit periods
  WYPIN_CLK         = 2                 ' clocks from the GETCT that takes the write instant to the WYPIN that writes
  PIN_LAT_ALLOW     = 12                ' ASSUMED allowance (not measured) for the WYPIN-to-smart-pin latency jitter; bound check in control W
  DUR_TOL_PCT       = 25                ' the duration window is expected +/- 25 %

  NBYTES            = 4                 ' data bytes of the framed packet (after SOP)
  NTX               = NBYTES + 1        ' values written: SOP then the data
  NSHORT            = 2                 ' data bytes of the short packet (arm N1)
  TX0               = $A5
  TX1               = $5A
  TX2               = $3C
  TX3               = $C3

  BIT_SOP           = $10               ' status [4] SOP in
  BIT_EOP           = $20               ' status [5] EOP in
  BIT_TOG           = $80               ' status [7] byte toggle
  BASE_MASK         = $30               ' baseline must have neither SOP in nor EOP in
  LINE_MASK         = %111              ' status [2] SE0 in, [1] K in, [0] J in (silicon-doc-text.txt:4627-:4629) -- the JUDGED line state
  J_STATE           = %001              ' line state J: J in, K in and SE0 in clear (gates JS, JB, JL, JD)
  BIT_STEADY        = %1000             ' status [3] steady state (:4626) -- printed only, never judged
  #0, J_PATH_FS, J_PATH_LS, J_PATH_D2  ' where an arm's J miss goes (j_path): RIG FAIL / gate JL / gates JL and JD

CON ' ---- Configuration Words ----
  CFG_DRIVE         = $0000_0076        ' P_USB_PAIR | P_OE  (D data %1_11011_0)
  SYM_DRIVE         = P_USB_PAIR | P_OE

  ' WRPIN D field positions and widths (silicon-doc-text.txt:3731)
  SHIFT_AAAA        = 28
  MASK_AAAA         = %1111
  SHIFT_BBBB        = 24
  MASK_BBBB         = %1111
  SHIFT_FFF         = 21
  MASK_FFF          = %111
  SHIFT_M           = 8
  MASK_M            = %1_1111_1111_1111
  SHIFT_TT          = 6
  MASK_TT           = %11
  SHIFT_SSSSS       = 1
  MASK_SSSSS        = %1_1111
  MASK_BIT0         = 1
  SSSSS_USB         = %11011
  TT_ON             = %01

CON ' ---- Mailbox Layout ----
  ' arms, in the order the engine runs them within a round
  ' ARM_SCF / ARM_SCL / ARM_SB / ARM_SC = the SHORT-settle arms (no WX_CLK wait between the WXPIN site and the baseline)
  #0, ARM_CFS, ARM_CLS, ARM_A1, ARM_A2, ARM_B, ARM_C, ARM_D1, ARM_D2, ARM_N1, ARM_N2, ARM_X, ARM_SCF, ARM_SCL, ARM_SB, ARM_SC
  NARMS             = 15

  LOGMAX            = 24                ' status-change events kept per arm

  ' per arm record (ARMW longs), written by the engine in this order:
  '  A_BASE   baseline status word (RQPIN of DM after the settle and AKPIN, before the packet)
  '  A_T0     CT at the SOP write (arm N2: CT at the start of the routine)
  '  A_LATE   largest lateness (write instant - deadline, clocks) over the data-byte writes
  '  A_W1..A_W4  write instant of data byte 1..4 relative to the SOP write (0 = not written)
  '  A_NLOG   status-change events seen (may exceed LOGMAX)
  '  A_LAST   last status word read, at the end of the window
  '  A_STL    settle: clocks from the GETCT at the WXPIN site (taken in EVERY arm, before the WXPIN if the arm has one) to the SOP write
  '  A_JW     clocks the DP IN took to be seen high before the J write (NO_TIME = the JW_TIMEOUT_CLK bound ran out)
  '  A_JST    status word after the J drive and its settle, read just before the GETCT at the WXPIN site
  '  A_SEEN   OR of every status word sampled in the observation window and NOT logged as an event (cog 0 ORs in the logged ones)
  '  A_LOG    LOGMAX pairs (status word of bits 4..15 changing, CT at the sample)
  #0, A_BASE, A_T0, A_LATE, A_W1, A_W2, A_W3, A_W4, A_NLOG, A_LAST, A_STL, A_JW, A_JST, A_SEEN, A_LOG
  ARMW              = A_LOG + LOGMAX * 2
  ROUNDW            = NARMS * ARMW

  MB_DONE           = 0
  MB_PROG           = 1
  MB_RES            = 2
  MB_LONGS          = MB_RES + ROUNDS * ROUNDW

  ' decoded record per arm and round (made by cog 0 from the log)
  #0, D_N, D_B0, D_B1, D_B2, D_B3, D_TSOP, D_TB1, D_TB4, D_DUR, D_TEOP, D_OVF
  DF                = 11

CON ' ---- Report Parameters ----
  LINE_MAX          = 700
  DEC_TOP           = 1_000_000_000
  DEC_DIGITS        = 10
  DEC_BASE          = 10
  HEX_DIGITS        = 8
  HEX_BITS          = 4
  HEX_GROUP         = 4
  HEX_LETTERS       = 10

VAR ' ---- Mailbox & Analysis State ----
  long mailbox[MB_LONGS]
  long dec[ROUNDS * NARMS * DF]
  long cnt[NARMS]
  long fail_count
  byte lineBuf[LINE_MAX + 1]
  long linePos


PUB main() | engineCog, startTick
'' Run the J-035 rig: check the constants, start the measuring cog, wait for its done flag, report.
''

' Local Variables:
' @local engineCog - cog id COGINIT returned (negative = no free cog)
' @local startTick - CT when the wait for the done flag began

  longfill(@mailbox, 0, MB_LONGS)
  mailbox[MB_PROG] := 0
  fail_count := 0
  linePos := 0
  say(string("=== VO-J-035: USB %11011 -- does a WXPIN on a RUNNING pair take effect and leave the next packet intact? ==="))
  ln_s(string("pair DM = P"))
  ln_u(PIN_DM)
  ln_s(string(", DP = P"))
  ln_u(PIN_DP)
  ln_s(string(" (NOTHING attached; both are driven); host; FS X = "))
  ln_h(X_FS)
  ln_s(string(", LS X = "))
  ln_h(X_LS)
  ln_s(string("; "))
  ln_u(ROUNDS)
  say(string(" rounds"))
  print_predictions()
  say(string(" "))
  say(string("--- CONTROL W: configuration words and schedule constants, decoded / recomputed ---"))
  check_rig_constants()
  if fail_count <> 0
    say(string("NO VERDICT: a construction control failed (see RIG FAIL lines above); the measuring cog was not started"))
  else
    engineCog := coginit(COGEXEC_NEW, @engine, @mailbox)
    if engineCog < 0
      say(string("RIG FAIL: no free cog for the measuring engine"))
      say(string("NO VERDICT: nothing was measured"))
    else
      startTick := getct()
      repeat until mailbox[MB_DONE] == MBOX_DONE or (getct() - startTick) > clkfreq * RIG_TIMEOUT_SEC
      if mailbox[MB_DONE] <> MBOX_DONE
        cogstop(engineCog)
        pinclear(PIN_DM addpins 1)
        ln_s(string("RIG FAIL: measuring cog did not report done within "))
        ln_u(RIG_TIMEOUT_SEC)
        ln_s(string(" s; it was in round "))
        ln_u(mailbox[MB_PROG])
        say(string("; the pair was cleared by cog 0"))
        say(string("NO VERDICT: the measurement did not complete"))
      else
        ln_s(string("measuring cog = "))
        ln_u(engineCog)
        say(string(" (debugger restricted to cog 0)"))
        report()
  finish()


PRI finish()
' Every terminal path ends here. pnut-term-ts ends the run when
' DEBUG_END_SESSION appears in the output (--headless or --exit-on-end-session).
'

  say(string("=== END ==="))
  debug("DEBUG_END_SESSION")
  repeat

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' line output.  Text lives in hub (string(...)), not in DEBUG data: each line
' is built in lineBuf and sent by the single debug() record in ln_out().
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRI ln_c(ch)
' Append one character to the line (cut at LINE_MAX).
'
' @param ch - the character

  if linePos < LINE_MAX
    lineBuf[linePos++] := ch

PRI ln_s(pStr) | ch
' Append a zero-terminated hub string to the line.
'
' @param pStr - hub address of the string

' Local Variables:
' @local ch - the character being copied

  repeat
    ch := byte[pStr++]
    if ch == 0
      quit
    ln_c(ch)

PRI ln_u(numVal) | divisor, digitVal, bStarted
' Append an unsigned decimal to the line.
'
' @param numVal - the number, read as unsigned

' Local Variables:
' @local divisor - the decimal place being printed
' @local digitVal - the digit at that place
' @local bStarted - a non-zero digit has been printed

  divisor := DEC_TOP
  bStarted := FALSE
  repeat DEC_DIGITS
    digitVal := numVal +/ divisor
    if digitVal <> 0 or bStarted or divisor == 1
      ln_c("0" + digitVal)
      bStarted := TRUE
    numVal := numVal +// divisor
    divisor /= DEC_BASE

PRI ln_t(timeVal)
' Append a time / duration in clocks: the word none for the NO_TIME sentinel (a value that never filled), else a decimal
' (with a sign if negative, so a real -1 prints as -1).
'
' @param timeVal - the value

  if timeVal == NO_TIME
    ln_s(string("none"))
  elseif timeVal < 0
    ln_c("-")
    ln_u(-timeVal)
  else
    ln_u(timeVal)

PRI ln_h(numVal) | idx, nibble
' Append a long in hex as $XXXX_XXXX.
'
' @param numVal - the number

' Local Variables:
' @local idx - nibble index, 7 (top) .. 0
' @local nibble - the nibble value

  ln_c("$")
  repeat idx from HEX_DIGITS - 1 to 0
    nibble := (numVal >> (idx * HEX_BITS)) & $F
    if nibble < HEX_LETTERS
      ln_c("0" + nibble)
    else
      ln_c("A" + nibble - HEX_LETTERS)
    if idx == HEX_GROUP
      ln_c("_")

PRI ln_bin(numVal, digits) | idx
' Append "%" and the low digits bits of a number.
'
' @param numVal - the number
' @param digits - how many bits

' Local Variables:
' @local idx - bit index

  ln_c("%")
  repeat idx from digits - 1 to 0
    ln_c("0" + ((numVal >> idx) & 1))

PRI ln_out()
' Send the line built so far as one debug() record and start a new line.
'

  lineBuf[linePos] := 0
  debug(zstr_(@lineBuf))
  linePos := 0

PRI say(pStr)
' Append a string and send the line.
'
' @param pStr - hub address of the string

  ln_s(pStr)
  ln_out()

PRI yes_no(bFlag) : pText
' Printable YES / NO.
'
' @param bFlag - TRUE or FALSE
' @returns pText - address of "YES" or "NO"

  if bFlag
    pText := string("YES")
  else
    pText := string("NO")

PRI arm_name(armIdx) : pText
' Printable arm name.
'
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X
' @returns pText - address of the name

  case armIdx
    ARM_CFS: pText := string("CFS control FS            ")
    ARM_CLS: pText := string("CLS control LS            ")
    ARM_A1:  pText := string("A1  WXPIN FS->FS same     ")
    ARM_A2:  pText := string("A2  WXPIN LS->LS same     ")
    ARM_B:   pText := string("B   WXPIN FS->LS          ")
    ARM_C:   pText := string("C   WXPIN LS->FS          ")
    ARM_D1:  pText := string("D1  WXPIN FS->LS (middle) ")
    ARM_D2:  pText := string("D2  WXPIN LS->FS (back)   ")
    ARM_N1:  pText := string("N1  negative: short (2)   ")
    ARM_N2:  pText := string("N2  negative: none sent   ")
    ARM_X:   pText := string("X   negative: FS, LS sched")
    ARM_SCF: pText := string("SCF control FS short      ")
    ARM_SCL: pText := string("SCL control LS short      ")
    ARM_SB:  pText := string("SB  WXPIN FS->LS short    ")
    ARM_SC:  pText := string("SC  WXPIN LS->FS short    ")
    other:   pText := string("?                          ")

PRI arm_fast(armIdx) : bFast
' The speed an arm's packet is expected to be received at.
'
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_D2, ARM_SCF..ARM_SC
' @returns bFast - TRUE = full-speed, FALSE = low-speed

  bFast := armIdx == ARM_CFS or armIdx == ARM_A1 or armIdx == ARM_C or armIdx == ARM_D2 or armIdx == ARM_SCF or armIdx == ARM_SC

PRI is_packet_arm(armIdx) : bPkt
' The arms whose requirement is a GOOD packet (everything except the negative controls N1, N2, X).
'
' @param armIdx - arm index
' @returns bPkt - TRUE when the arm is a GOOD-packet arm

  bPkt := armIdx <> ARM_N1 and armIdx <> ARM_N2 and armIdx <> ARM_X

PRI judged(armIdx, bLs, bGateS, bGateJd) : bJ
' Whether an arm is a JUDGED arm in this run.  A1 always; D2 needs gate JD (its own J before its WXPIN site read J);
' A2, B, C, D1 need the low-speed half (gate L: control CLS GOOD, AND gate JL: the low-speed J read J);
' SB and SC need the low-speed half and gate S (the short-settle controls SCF and SCL).  The controls and negatives are gates, not judged arms.
'
' @param armIdx - arm index
' @param bLs - the low-speed half holds: gate L and gate JL
' @param bGateS - gate S holds
' @param bGateJd - gate JD holds (D2's J read J in every round)
' @returns bJ - TRUE when the arm is judged

  case armIdx
    ARM_A1: bJ := TRUE
    ARM_D2: bJ := bGateJd
    ARM_A2, ARM_B, ARM_C, ARM_D1: bJ := bLs
    ARM_SB, ARM_SC: bJ := bLs and bGateS
    other: bJ := FALSE

PRI clk_bits(nBits, baudVal) : clks
' Clocks in nBits bit periods at a baud word: (nBits * 65536 + baud / 2) / baud, i.e. rounded to the nearest clock.
'
' @param nBits - bit periods
' @param baudVal - the 14-bit baud word
' @returns clks - clocks

  clks := (nBits * 65536 + baudVal / 2) / baudVal

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' pre-registered readings and construction controls
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRI print_predictions()
' Print the readings fixed before the run.
'

  say(string("PRE-REGISTERED READINGS (fixed before the run):"))
  say(string("  SCOPE: what is proved is the BAUD (WXPIN D[13:0]): the baud set by the WXPIN is in force and the next packet is intact. The FS/LS bit D[14] is NOT observable on this self-sensing pair"))
  say(string("    (the pair's receiver reads its own output in the same speed convention; the status line-state bits 0..3 cannot show the pad polarity; no other observer is in the rig) -- no verdict says 'FS/LS mode changed'"))
  say(string("  each arm = fresh start, J driven (WYPIN #3 after a bounded wait for the DP IN) and settled 4000 clocks, [GETCT at the WXPIN site, WXPIN step], settle,"))
  say(string("    AKPIN + RQPIN baseline, ONE packet SOP,$A5,$5A,$3C,$C3 ; D2 runs on D1's pair and drives the J again before its own WXPIN site"))
  say(string("  LINE STATE = status bits 2..0 (SE0 in, K in, J in; :4627-:4629); J = %001. Bit 3 (steady, :4626) is PRINTED, never judged."))
  say(string("    The DP-IN wait before each J write is PRINTED, never judged: a timeout (jw timeout) is an observation; the J is written either way"))
  say(string("  J BEFORE THE WXPIN SITE (jst, gate JS) and the CONTROLS' BASELINE (gate JB) must read J. Where a miss goes depends on the speed the J was driven at:"))
  say(string("    full-speed start (CFS A1 B D1 N1 N2 X SCF SB): RIG FAIL ; low-speed start (CLS A2 C SCL SC): gate JL fails = the low-speed arms A2 B C D1 SB SC NOT JUDGED ;"))
  say(string("    D2 (J driven on D1's low-speed pair): gates JL and JD fail = the low-speed arms AND D2 NOT JUDGED"))
  say(string("  the WXPIN arms' baseline (A1 A2 B C D1 D2 SB SC) is PRINTED ONLY: expected J of the speed in force (:4602, :4585, :4610); the Documentation does not say a held state follows a WXPIN"))
  say(string("  SETTLE between the WXPIN site and the baseline: LONG arms (CFS CLS A1 A2 B C D1 D2 N1 N2 X) wait 20000 clocks (100 us) ;"))
  say(string("    SHORT arms (SCF SCL SB SC) wait nothing: WXPIN, then the code path (~110-120 clocks by instruction count, measured per arm as stl, gate Q <= 300)"))
  say(string("    SCF / SCL = the controls of SB / SC: the same code and timing with no WXPIN (2 clocks less) ; CFS / CLS = the controls of the long arms"))
  say(string("  value k is written at a FIXED deadline: k=1 at 4 bit periods after the SOP write, then every 8 bit periods (FS 67 then +133 clocks, LS 534 then +1068); no IN flag is read"))
  say(string("  GOOD round of a packet arm = baseline has SOP-in and EOP-in clear ; n=4 bytes $A5 $5A $3C $C3 in order, counted between SOP-in and EOP-in"))
  say(string("    and first->fourth byte time within +/-25 % of the speed's expected 24 bit periods (FS 400, LS 3203 clocks). EOP-in and the error bit are printed, not part of GOOD"))
  say(string("  arm class ALL = GOOD in all rounds, NONE = in no round, MIXED = otherwise. Judged arms: A1 ; D2 when gate JD holds ; A2, B, C, D1 when gates L and JL hold ;"))
  say(string("    SB, SC when gates L, JL and S hold"))
  say(string("  GATES (failure = RIG FAIL + NO VERDICT): W constants ; JS / JB full-speed start: line J before the WXPIN site (every FS-start arm) and at every FS-start control's baseline"))
  say(string("    P every write within 48 clocks of its deadline, in every arm ; O no event log overflow ; K arm CFS GOOD every round ; Q settle bands"))
  say(string("    N1 short packet = exactly n=2 $A5 $5A ; N2 nothing sent = exactly n=0 ; X FS pair written at the LS schedule = NOT intact, every round"))
  say(string("  GATE L (not a RIG FAIL): arm CLS (low-speed, no WXPIN) GOOD every round ; if it fails the low-speed arms A2, B, C, D1, SB, SC are NOT JUDGED"))
  say(string("  GATE JL (not a RIG FAIL): every J driven at low speed (CLS A2 C SCL SC D2 before the site, CLS SCL at the baseline) reads J ; if it fails the low-speed arms are NOT JUDGED"))
  say(string("  GATE JD (not a RIG FAIL): D2's J before its WXPIN site reads J every round ; if it fails D2 is NOT JUDGED"))
  say(string("  GATE S (not a RIG FAIL): arms SCF and SCL (short settle, no WXPIN) GOOD every round ; if it fails the short-settle arms SB, SC are NOT JUDGED"))
  say(string("  SURVIVES     : RIG-FAIL gates hold, gates L, JL, JD and S hold, every judged arm ALL -- after a WXPIN on a running pair (same word, FS<->LS, and back;"))
  say(string("    after a long and after a short settle) the baud set by the WXPIN is in force and the next packet is intact"))
  say(string("  DISTURBED    : RIG-FAIL gates hold (L, JL, JD, S may not: their arms are then unjudged and named) and at least one judged arm NONE (named, with MIXED ones) -- the packet after that WXPIN step was not GOOD in any round"))
  say(string("    B, D1, SB (an FS pair given the LS schedule): an ignored WXPIN reads like control X (n<4, early EOP-in, not GOOD)"))
  say(string("    C, D2, SC (an LS pair given the FS schedule): overrun is undocumented (:4608); the arm is not GOOD by count or duration, and a WYPIN stall trips gate P = NO VERDICT"))
  say(string("    A1, A2 re-issue the same word, so an ignored re-issue cannot be told from a taken one -- they prove only that the re-issue does not disturb the packet"))
  say(string("  INCONCLUSIVE : RIG-FAIL gates hold, no judged arm NONE, and some judged arm MIXED, or every judged arm ALL but gate L, JL, JD or S failed (those arms named, not judged)"))
  say(string("  NO VERDICT   : gate W, JS / JB (full-speed start), P, O, K, N1, N2, X or Q failed, or the measuring cog did not finish ; raw records are printed in every case"))
  say(string("  every raw line also prints jst (line before the WXPIN site), base (before the packet) and seen (line states of the window): whether the line was J, and whether the transmitter drove it (K seen)"))

PRI check_rig_constants() | baudFs, baudLs
' Control W: the pair lies in the free set and is even/odd; the drive word decodes to the published fields and
' equals its Spin2 symbol composition; both WXPIN words equal the FRAC computation; the schedule constants equal
' bit-period counts at the run-time baud words, the late margin is below the under-run bound, the windows are disjoint.
'

' Local Variables:
' @local baudFs - (12_000_000 FRAC clkfreq) >> 16 computed at run time
' @local baudLs - (1_500_000 FRAC clkfreq) >> 16 computed at run time

  if (PIN_DM & 1) <> 0 or PIN_DP <> PIN_DM + 1 or PAIR <> PIN_DM | (1 << 6)
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the pair is not an even/odd pair addressed as DM + one additional pin"))
    fail_count++
  if PIN_DM > PIN_MAX_FREE_LOW and (PIN_DM < 32 or PIN_DM > 46)
    say(string("RIG FAIL: DM is outside the free set (P0..P7, P32..P47)"))
    fail_count++
  check_word(string("drive   "), CFG_DRIVE, SYM_DRIVE, TT_ON)
  baudFs := (RATE_FS frac clkfreq) >> 16
  baudLs := (RATE_LS frac clkfreq) >> 16
  check_xword(string("FS"), X_FS, baudFs, 1)
  check_xword(string("LS"), X_LS, baudLs, 0)
  check_sched(string("FS"), baudFs, G0_FS, STEP_FS, DUR_FS, OBS_FS)
  check_sched(string("LS"), baudLs, G0_LS, STEP_LS, DUR_LS, OBS_LS)
  ln_s(string("  late margin "))
  ln_u(LATE_MARGIN)
  ln_s(string(" + GETCT-to-WYPIN "))
  ln_u(WYPIN_CLK)
  ln_s(string(" + assumed pin-latency allowance "))
  ln_u(PIN_LAT_ALLOW)
  ln_s(string(" = "))
  ln_u(LATE_MARGIN + WYPIN_CLK + PIN_LAT_ALLOW)
  ln_s(string(" clocks vs under-run bound (4 FS bit periods = "))
  ln_u(G0_FS)
  ln_s(string(" rounded; 66.7 exact) ; duration windows FS "))
  ln_u(DUR_FS * (100 - DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100)
  ln_c("-")
  ln_u(DUR_FS * (100 + DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100)
  ln_s(string(" , LS "))
  ln_u(DUR_LS * (100 - DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100)
  ln_c("-")
  ln_u(DUR_LS * (100 + DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100)
  ln_out()
  if LATE_MARGIN + WYPIN_CLK + PIN_LAT_ALLOW >= G0_FS - 1
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the late margin plus the GETCT-to-WYPIN time plus the pin-latency allowance is not below the under-run bound (4 full-speed bit periods, 66.7 clocks)"))
    fail_count++
  if DUR_FS * (100 + DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100 >= DUR_LS * (100 - DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the full-speed and low-speed duration windows overlap"))
    fail_count++
  if OBS_BITS <= (SCHED_G0_BITS + (NBYTES - 1) * SCHED_STEP_BITS) + 8 + 3 + 16
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the observation window does not cover the last write, its byte, the EOP and a margin"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  J drive before the WXPIN site: settle "))
  ln_u(J_SETTLE_CLK)
  ln_s(string(" clocks vs 7 bit periods (7 bit periods of J clear SOP-in / EOP-in, :4624-:4625) FS "))
  ln_u(clk_bits(STEADY_BITS, baudFs))
  ln_s(string(" , LS "))
  ln_u(clk_bits(STEADY_BITS, baudLs))
  ln_s(string(" ; bound of the DP-IN wait "))
  ln_u(JW_TIMEOUT_CLK)
  ln_s(string(" clocks"))
  ln_out()
  if J_SETTLE_CLK < clk_bits(STEADY_BITS, baudFs) or J_SETTLE_CLK < clk_bits(STEADY_BITS, baudLs)
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the J settle is shorter than 7 bit periods at one of the speeds -- SOP-in / EOP-in could not have cleared before the WXPIN site"))
    fail_count++

PRI check_sched(pLabel, baudRun, g0Const, stepConst, durConst, obsConst)
' Print one speed's schedule constants beside the run-time bit-period count and count a construction failure if they differ.
'
' @param pLabel - label string
' @param baudRun - the FRAC computation at run time
' @param g0Const - G0 constant
' @param stepConst - STEP constant
' @param durConst - DUR constant
' @param obsConst - OBS constant

  ln_s(string("  schedule "))
  ln_s(pLabel)
  ln_s(string(" (clocks: constant / computed): first gap "))
  ln_u(g0Const)
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(clk_bits(SCHED_G0_BITS, baudRun))
  ln_s(string(" , step "))
  ln_u(stepConst)
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(clk_bits(SCHED_STEP_BITS, baudRun))
  ln_s(string(" , duration "))
  ln_u(durConst)
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(clk_bits(DUR_BITS, baudRun))
  ln_s(string(" , window "))
  ln_u(obsConst)
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(clk_bits(OBS_BITS, baudRun))
  ln_out()
  if g0Const <> clk_bits(SCHED_G0_BITS, baudRun) or stepConst <> clk_bits(SCHED_STEP_BITS, baudRun) or durConst <> clk_bits(DUR_BITS, baudRun) or obsConst <> clk_bits(OBS_BITS, baudRun)
    ln_s(string("RIG FAIL: the "))
    ln_s(pLabel)
    say(string(" schedule constants differ from the bit-period counts at this clkfreq"))
    fail_count++

PRI check_xword(pLabel, xWord, baudRun, bFast)
' Decode one WXPIN word, print it, and count a construction failure if it differs from host + speed + the FRAC baud.
'
' @param pLabel - label string
' @param xWord - the word the measuring cog writes
' @param baudRun - the FRAC computation at run time
' @param bFast - 1 for full-speed (D[14] = 1), 0 for low-speed

  ln_s(string("  WXPIN word "))
  ln_s(pLabel)
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_h(xWord)
  ln_s(string(": D[15] host="))
  ln_u((xWord >> 15) & 1)
  ln_s(string(" D[14] full-speed="))
  ln_u((xWord >> 14) & 1)
  ln_s(string(" D[13:0] baud="))
  ln_u(xWord & $3FFF)
  ln_s(string("  FRAC computation at "))
  ln_u(clkfreq)
  ln_s(string(" Hz = "))
  ln_u(baudRun)
  ln_out()
  if (xWord >> 15) <> 1 or ((xWord >> 14) & 1) <> bFast or (xWord & $3FFF) <> baudRun or (baudRun >> 12) <> 0
    ln_s(string("RIG FAIL: the WXPIN word "))
    ln_s(pLabel)
    say(string(" does not hold host + its speed bit + the computed baud with the two MSBs 0"))
    fail_count++

PRI check_word(pLabel, cfgWord, symWord, expTt)
' Decode one WRPIN word into its published fields, print them, and count a
' construction failure if any field or the symbol composition disagrees.
'
' @param pLabel - label string for the printed line
' @param cfgWord - the configuration word the measuring cog writes
' @param symWord - the same word composed from the Spin2 v55 symbols
' @param expTt - expected %TT

  ln_s(string("  "))
  ln_s(pLabel)
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_h(cfgWord)
  ln_s(string(": AAAA="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_AAAA) & MASK_AAAA, 4)
  ln_s(string(" BBBB="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_BBBB) & MASK_BBBB, 4)
  ln_s(string(" FFF="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_FFF) & MASK_FFF, 3)
  ln_s(string(" M="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_M) & MASK_M, 13)
  ln_s(string(" TT="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_TT) & MASK_TT, 2)
  ln_s(string(" SSSSS="))
  ln_bin((cfgWord >> SHIFT_SSSSS) & MASK_SSSSS, 5)
  ln_s(string(" bit0="))
  ln_u(cfgWord & MASK_BIT0)
  ln_s(string("  symbols="))
  ln_h(symWord)
  ln_out()
  if ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_AAAA) & MASK_AAAA) <> 0 or ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_BBBB) & MASK_BBBB) <> 0 or ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_FFF) & MASK_FFF) <> 0 or ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_M) & MASK_M) <> 0 or ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_TT) & MASK_TT) <> expTt or ((cfgWord >> SHIFT_SSSSS) & MASK_SSSSS) <> SSSSS_USB or (cfgWord & MASK_BIT0) <> 0
    ln_s(string("RIG FAIL: "))
    ln_s(pLabel)
    say(string(" fields differ from the expected AAAA/BBBB/FFF/M = 0, TT, SSSSS = %11011, bit0 = 0"))
    fail_count++
  if cfgWord <> symWord
    ln_s(string("RIG FAIL: "))
    ln_s(pLabel)
    ln_s(string(" word "))
    ln_h(cfgWord)
    ln_s(string(" differs from its Spin2 symbol composition "))
    ln_h(symWord)
    ln_out()
    fail_count++

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' report
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRI rec(roundIdx, armIdx, slotIdx) : recVal
' One recorded long of an arm in a round.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X
' @param slotIdx - A_BASE..A_LOG+2*LOGMAX-1
' @returns recVal - the recorded long

  recVal := mailbox[MB_RES + roundIdx * ROUNDW + armIdx * ARMW + slotIdx]

PRI dget(roundIdx, armIdx, fldIdx) : decVal
' One decoded field of an arm in a round.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X
' @param fldIdx - D_N..D_OVF
' @returns decVal - the decoded value

  decVal := dec[(roundIdx * NARMS + armIdx) * DF + fldIdx]

PRI decode(roundIdx, armIdx) | rb, db, nUse, evIdx, stv, tv, t0v, bInSop, bInEop, prevTg, nb
' Decode one arm's event log into the decoded record.  The log holds every change of status bits 4..15 (SOP-in, EOP-in,
' error, byte toggle, last byte) seen by the polling loop, in time order, as (status, CT) pairs.
'   SOP-in rising (before any EOP-in)  opens the packet; the toggle bit at that moment is the reference.
'   A change of the toggle bit while the packet is open and EOP-in has not been seen = one received byte (n counts all of them).
'   EOP-in rising closes the packet for good: nothing after it is counted.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X

' Local Variables:
' @local rb - mailbox index of the arm record
' @local db - dec[] index of the decoded record
' @local nUse - events decoded (capped at LOGMAX)
' @local evIdx - event index
' @local stv - status word of the event
' @local tv - event time relative to A_T0
' @local t0v - A_T0
' @local bInSop - SOP-in has been seen
' @local bInEop - EOP-in has been seen
' @local prevTg - the toggle bit last accounted for
' @local nb - bytes counted

  rb := MB_RES + roundIdx * ROUNDW + armIdx * ARMW
  db := (roundIdx * NARMS + armIdx) * DF
  dec[db + D_N] := 0
  dec[db + D_B0] := NO_BYTE
  dec[db + D_B1] := NO_BYTE
  dec[db + D_B2] := NO_BYTE
  dec[db + D_B3] := NO_BYTE
  dec[db + D_TSOP] := NO_TIME
  dec[db + D_TB1] := NO_TIME
  dec[db + D_TB4] := NO_TIME
  dec[db + D_DUR] := NO_TIME
  dec[db + D_TEOP] := NO_TIME
  dec[db + D_OVF] := FALSE
  nUse := mailbox[rb + A_NLOG]
  if nUse > LOGMAX
    dec[db + D_OVF] := TRUE
    nUse := LOGMAX
  t0v := mailbox[rb + A_T0]
  bInSop := FALSE
  bInEop := FALSE
  prevTg := 0
  nb := 0
  evIdx := 0
  repeat while evIdx < nUse
    stv := mailbox[rb + A_LOG + evIdx * 2]
    tv := mailbox[rb + A_LOG + evIdx * 2 + 1] - t0v
    if bInSop and bInEop == FALSE and (stv & BIT_TOG) <> prevTg
      prevTg := stv & BIT_TOG
      nb++
      if nb <= NBYTES
        dec[db + D_B0 + nb - 1] := (stv >> 8) & $FF
      if nb == 1
        dec[db + D_TB1] := tv
      if nb == NBYTES
        dec[db + D_TB4] := tv
    if bInEop == FALSE and (stv & BIT_EOP) <> 0
      bInEop := TRUE
      dec[db + D_TEOP] := tv
    if bInSop == FALSE and bInEop == FALSE and (stv & BIT_SOP) <> 0
      bInSop := TRUE
      prevTg := stv & BIT_TOG
      dec[db + D_TSOP] := tv
    evIdx++
  dec[db + D_N] := nb
  if dec[db + D_TB1] <> NO_TIME and dec[db + D_TB4] <> NO_TIME
    dec[db + D_DUR] := dec[db + D_TB4] - dec[db + D_TB1]

PRI base_clean(roundIdx, armIdx) : bClean
' Whether the baseline status read before the packet has neither SOP-in nor EOP-in set.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - arm
' @returns bClean - TRUE when bits 4 and 5 of A_BASE are clear

  bClean := (rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE) & BASE_MASK) == 0

PRI bytes_intact(roundIdx, armIdx) : bOk
' Whether the decoded packet is n = 4 with the bytes $A5 $5A $3C $C3 in order.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - arm
' @returns bOk - TRUE when intact

  bOk := dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_N) == NBYTES and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B0) == TX0 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B1) == TX1 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B2) == TX2 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B3) == TX3

PRI dur_ok(durVal, bFast) : bOk
' Whether a measured first->fourth byte duration is within DUR_TOL_PCT of the speed's expected duration.
'
' @param durVal - the duration in clocks, or NO_TIME
' @param bFast - TRUE = full-speed expected, FALSE = low-speed
' @returns bOk - TRUE when inside the window

  if bFast
    bOk := durVal <> NO_TIME and durVal >= DUR_FS * (100 - DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100 and durVal <= DUR_FS * (100 + DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100
  else
    bOk := durVal <> NO_TIME and durVal >= DUR_LS * (100 - DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100 and durVal <= DUR_LS * (100 + DUR_TOL_PCT) / 100

PRI dur_window(durVal) : pText
' Which speed's duration window a measured duration falls in (diagnostic, printed on every raw line).
'
' @param durVal - the duration in clocks, or NO_TIME
' @returns pText - address of "FS", "LS" or "none"

  if dur_ok(durVal, TRUE)
    pText := string("FS")
  elseif dur_ok(durVal, FALSE)
    pText := string("LS")
  else
    pText := string("none")

PRI count_other(armIdx) : nOther | roundIdx
' For a GOOD-packet arm: in how many rounds the measured duration fell in the OTHER speed's window than the arm requires.
'
' @param armIdx - a GOOD-packet arm
' @returns nOther - rounds whose duration is in the other speed's window

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round

  nOther := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    if dur_ok(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_DUR), arm_fast(armIdx) == FALSE)
      nOther++

PRI stl_lim(firstArm, lastArm, bMax) : limVal | roundIdx, armIdx, stlVal
' The smallest or largest settle (A_STL) over a range of arms and all rounds.
'
' @param firstArm - first arm index
' @param lastArm - last arm index
' @param bMax - TRUE = largest, FALSE = smallest
' @returns limVal - the limit, in clocks

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm
' @local stlVal - the settle of one record

  limVal := -1
  repeat armIdx from firstArm to lastArm
    repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
      stlVal := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_STL)
      if limVal == -1 or (bMax and stlVal > limVal) or (bMax == FALSE and stlVal < limVal)
        limVal := stlVal

PRI ln_settle()
' Append the settle every arm used, to the verdict line being built.
'

  ln_s(string(" | settle WXPIN-site to SOP write, clocks: long arms CFS..X "))
  ln_u(stl_lim(ARM_CFS, ARM_X, FALSE))
  ln_s(string(".."))
  ln_u(stl_lim(ARM_CFS, ARM_X, TRUE))
  ln_s(string(" (= a "))
  ln_u(WX_CLK)
  ln_s(string("-clock wait + the code path) ; short arms SCF SCL SB SC "))
  ln_u(stl_lim(ARM_SCF, ARM_SC, FALSE))
  ln_s(string(".."))
  ln_u(stl_lim(ARM_SCF, ARM_SC, TRUE))
  ln_s(string(" (the code path only, no wait)"))

PRI arm_ok(roundIdx, armIdx) : bOk
' Whether an arm's record in a round is what that arm is required to return.
' N1 / N2 / X are the negative controls: their required record is the SHORT / EMPTY / NOT-INTACT one.
' Every other arm is GOOD: clean baseline, the full packet in order, at its expected speed.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_SC
' @returns bOk - TRUE when the record is the required one

  case armIdx
    ARM_N1:
      bOk := base_clean(roundIdx, armIdx) and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_N) == NSHORT and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B0) == TX0 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B1) == TX1 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B2) == NO_BYTE and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B3) == NO_BYTE
    ARM_N2:
      bOk := base_clean(roundIdx, armIdx) and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_N) == 0 and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B0) == NO_BYTE and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B1) == NO_BYTE and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B2) == NO_BYTE and dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B3) == NO_BYTE
    ARM_X:
      bOk := base_clean(roundIdx, armIdx) and bytes_intact(roundIdx, armIdx) == FALSE
    other:
      bOk := base_clean(roundIdx, armIdx) and bytes_intact(roundIdx, armIdx) and dur_ok(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_DUR), arm_fast(armIdx))

PRI arm_class(armIdx) : pText
' Class of an arm from its count of required rounds.
'
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X
' @returns pText - ALL, NONE or MIXED

  if cnt[armIdx] == ROUNDS
    pText := string("ALL")
  elseif cnt[armIdx] == 0
    pText := string("NONE")
  else
    pText := string("MIXED")

PRI count_late() : nBad | roundIdx, armIdx, lateVal
' Gate P: print and count every arm record whose largest write lateness exceeds LATE_MARGIN (or is negative).
'
' @returns nBad - how many arm records are late

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm
' @local lateVal - A_LATE of the record

  nBad := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      lateVal := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LATE)
      if lateVal > LATE_MARGIN or lateVal < 0
        ln_s(string("  late write: r"))
        ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
        ln_c(" ")
        ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
        ln_s(string(" lateness "))
        ln_t(lateVal)
        ln_s(string(" clocks (margin "))
        ln_u(LATE_MARGIN)
        ln_c(")")
        ln_out()
        nBad++

PRI count_ovf() : nBad | roundIdx, armIdx
' Gate O: print and count every arm record whose event log overflowed.
'
' @returns nBad - how many arm records overflowed

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm

  nBad := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      if dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_OVF)
        ln_s(string("  log overflow: r"))
        ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
        ln_c(" ")
        ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
        ln_s(string(" events "))
        ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_NLOG))
        ln_s(string(" (kept "))
        ln_u(LOGMAX)
        ln_c(")")
        ln_out()
        nBad++

PRI count_settle() : nBad | roundIdx, armIdx, stlVal, bShort
' Gate Q: print and count every arm record whose settle (A_STL) is outside its band: a short-settle arm (SCF SCL SB SC) must be
' 0..SHORT_MAX_CLK ; every other arm must be WX_CLK..WX_CLK+SHORT_MAX_CLK (the long wait plus the same code path).
'
' @returns nBad - how many arm records are outside their band

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm
' @local stlVal - A_STL of the record
' @local bShort - the arm is a short-settle arm

  nBad := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      stlVal := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_STL)
      bShort := armIdx >= ARM_SCF
      if (bShort and (stlVal < 0 or stlVal > SHORT_MAX_CLK)) or (bShort == FALSE and (stlVal < WX_CLK or stlVal > WX_CLK + SHORT_MAX_CLK))
        ln_s(string("  settle out of band: r"))
        ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
        ln_c(" ")
        ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
        ln_s(string(" settle "))
        ln_t(stlVal)
        ln_s(string(" clocks (short arms 0.."))
        ln_u(SHORT_MAX_CLK)
        ln_s(string(", others "))
        ln_u(WX_CLK)
        ln_s(string(".."))
        ln_u(WX_CLK + SHORT_MAX_CLK)
        ln_c(")")
        ln_out()
        nBad++

PRI is_control(armIdx) : bCtl
' The control arms: the positive controls CFS, CLS, SCF, SCL and the negative controls N1, N2, X (no WXPIN step in any of them).
'
' @param armIdx - arm index
' @returns bCtl - TRUE for a control arm

  bCtl := armIdx == ARM_CFS or armIdx == ARM_CLS or armIdx == ARM_SCF or armIdx == ARM_SCL or armIdx == ARM_N1 or armIdx == ARM_N2 or armIdx == ARM_X

PRI j_path(armIdx) : pathVal
' Which speed an arm's J is driven at, and so where a J miss of that arm goes:
'   J_PATH_FS (0) -- full-speed start: CFS A1 B D1 N1 N2 X SCF SB.  A miss is a RIG FAIL (gate JS / JB).
'   J_PATH_LS (1) -- low-speed start: CLS A2 C SCL SC.  A miss fails gate JL: the low-speed arms are NOT JUDGED.
'   J_PATH_D2 (2) -- D2, whose J is driven on D1's pair after D1's WXPIN to LS.  A miss fails gates JL and JD: the low-speed arms
'                    and D2 are NOT JUDGED.
'
' @param armIdx - arm index
' @returns pathVal - J_PATH_FS, J_PATH_LS or J_PATH_D2

  case armIdx
    ARM_CLS, ARM_A2, ARM_C, ARM_SCL, ARM_SC: pathVal := J_PATH_LS
    ARM_D2: pathVal := J_PATH_D2
    other: pathVal := J_PATH_FS

PRI is_j(statVal) : bJ
' Whether a status word's judged line state (bits 2..0: SE0 in, K in, J in -- silicon-doc-text.txt:4627-:4629) is J (%001).
' Bit 3 (steady, :4626) is NOT part of it.
'
' @param statVal - a status word
' @returns bJ - TRUE when bits 2..0 = %001

  bJ := (statVal & LINE_MASK) == J_STATE

PRI base_j(roundIdx, armIdx) : bJ
' Whether the baseline status (read just before the packet) shows the line state J (bits 2..0 = %001).
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - arm
' @returns bJ - TRUE when the baseline line state is J

  bJ := is_j(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE))

PRI ln_line(statVal)
' Append a status word's line state: bits 2..0 (SE0 K J) and, separately, bit 3 (steady -- printed, not judged).
'
' @param statVal - a status word

  ln_s(string(" (SE0/K/J "))
  ln_bin(statVal, 3)
  ln_s(string(" steady "))
  ln_u((statVal & BIT_STEADY) >> 3)
  ln_c(")")

PRI line_seen(roundIdx, armIdx) : seenVal | evIdx, nUse
' The line states (bits 2..0: SE0 K J) seen by ANY sample of the packet window: the engine's OR of the samples it did not log
' (A_SEEN) ORed with the status word of every logged event (complete unless the log overflowed -- gate O).
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - arm
' @returns seenVal - OR of the line-state bits 2..0 of every sample

' Local Variables:
' @local evIdx - event index
' @local nUse - events kept

  seenVal := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_SEEN)
  nUse := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_NLOG)
  if nUse > LOGMAX
    nUse := LOGMAX
  evIdx := 0
  repeat while evIdx < nUse
    seenVal |= rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LOG + evIdx * 2)
    evIdx++
  seenVal &= LINE_MASK

PRI count_jw() : nBad | roundIdx, armIdx
' Observation (NOT a gate): count the arm records whose wait for the DP IN before the J write ran out (A_JW = NO_TIME).
' The J was written either way; each such record prints "jw timeout" on its raw line.
'
' @returns nBad - how many arm records timed out

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm

  nBad := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      if rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JW) == NO_TIME
        nBad++

PRI count_js() : nFs, nLs, nD2 | roundIdx, armIdx, stv
' Gate JS: print and count every arm record whose status after the J drive (before the WXPIN site) is not J (bits 2..0 = %001),
' split by the arm's J path (j_path).
'
' @returns nFs - misses in full-speed-start arms (RIG FAIL)
' @returns nLs - misses in low-speed-start arms (gate JL)
' @returns nD2 - misses in D2 (gates JL and JD)

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm
' @local stv - A_JST of the record

  nFs := 0
  nLs := 0
  nD2 := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      stv := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JST)
      if is_j(stv) == FALSE
        ln_s(string("  JS: r"))
        ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
        ln_c(" ")
        ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
        ln_s(string(" status after the J drive "))
        ln_h(stv)
        ln_line(stv)
        case j_path(armIdx)
          J_PATH_FS:
            ln_s(string(" -- not J; full-speed start: RIG FAIL"))
            nFs++
          J_PATH_LS:
            ln_s(string(" -- not J; low-speed start: gate JL"))
            nLs++
          other:
            ln_s(string(" -- not J; D2 on D1's low-speed pair: gates JL and JD"))
            nD2++
        ln_out()

PRI count_jb() : nFs, nLs | roundIdx, armIdx
' Gate JB: print and count every CONTROL arm record (CFS CLS SCF SCL N1 N2 X) whose baseline (just before the packet) is not J
' (bits 2..0 = %001), split by the arm's J path: CLS and SCL are low-speed starts, the others full-speed starts.
'
' @returns nFs - misses in full-speed-start controls (RIG FAIL)
' @returns nLs - misses in low-speed-start controls (gate JL)

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm

  nFs := 0
  nLs := 0
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      if is_control(armIdx) and base_j(roundIdx, armIdx) == FALSE
        ln_s(string("  JB: r"))
        ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
        ln_c(" ")
        ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
        ln_s(string(" control baseline "))
        ln_h(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE))
        ln_line(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE))
        if j_path(armIdx) == J_PATH_LS
          ln_s(string(" -- not J; low-speed start: gate JL"))
          nLs++
        else
          ln_s(string(" -- not J; full-speed start: RIG FAIL"))
          nFs++
        ln_out()

PRI report() | roundIdx, armIdx, bGateL, bGateS, bGateJl, bGateJd, bLs, nAll, nNone, nMixed, nLate, nOvf, nStl, nJw, nFsJs, nLsJs, nD2Js, nFsJb, nLsJb, nBj
' Decode, print the raw records, gate, decide. Single exit.
'

' Local Variables:
' @local roundIdx - round
' @local armIdx - arm
' @local bGateL - the low-speed control (CLS) was GOOD in every round
' @local bGateS - the short-settle controls (SCF and SCL) were GOOD in every round
' @local bGateJl - every low-speed J (JS of CLS A2 C SCL SC D2, JB of CLS SCL) read J
' @local bGateJd - D2's J (JS of D2) read J in every round
' @local bLs - the low-speed half holds: gate L and gate JL
' @local nStl - arm records whose settle is outside its band
' @local nAll - judged arms of class ALL
' @local nNone - judged arms of class NONE
' @local nMixed - judged arms of class MIXED
' @local nLate - arm records with a late write
' @local nOvf - arm records whose log overflowed
' @local nJw - arm records whose DP-IN wait before the J write ran out (printed observation, not a gate)
' @local nFsJs - full-speed-start arm records not J before the WXPIN site (gate JS: RIG FAIL)
' @local nLsJs - low-speed-start arm records not J before the WXPIN site (gate JL)
' @local nD2Js - D2 records not J before its WXPIN site (gates JL and JD)
' @local nFsJb - full-speed-start control records whose baseline is not J (gate JB: RIG FAIL)
' @local nLsJb - low-speed-start control records (CLS, SCL) whose baseline is not J (gate JL)
' @local nBj - rounds of one arm whose baseline is J (printed in the per-arm count line)

  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      decode(roundIdx, armIdx)
  say(string(" "))
  say(string("--- RAW RECORDS per round and arm (base = baseline status; n = bytes counted between SOP-in and EOP-in; bytes ---"))
  say(string("    sop / tb1 / tb4 / eop = SOP-in, first byte, fourth byte, EOP-in times in clocks after the SOP write ; dur = tb4 - tb1 ; late = largest write lateness"))
  say(string("    dwin = which speed's duration window dur falls in (FS / LS / none) ; stl = settle, clocks from the GETCT at the WXPIN site to the SOP write"))
  say(string("    w = the four data-byte write times after the SOP write ; ev = status-change events (bits 4..15) ; last = last status"))
  say(string("    jw = clocks until the DP IN was seen before the J write (timeout = the bound ran out; printed, not judged) ; jst = status after the J drive, before the WXPIN site"))
  say(string("    (SE0/K/J %bbb steady b) = line state of base / jst: bits 2..0 judged (J = %001), bit 3 printed only"))
  say(string("    seen = line states (SE0/K/J, bits 2..0) seen by any sample of the window: K seen = the transmitter drove the line ; req = the arm's required record met) ---"))
  repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      print_arm(roundIdx, armIdx)
  longfill(@cnt, 0, NARMS)
  repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
    repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
      if arm_ok(roundIdx, armIdx)
        cnt[armIdx]++
  say(string(" "))
  say(string("--- PER-ARM COUNT of rounds meeting the arm's requirement (GOOD; N1, N2: the short / empty record; X: not intact) ---"))
  repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
    ln_s(string("  "))
    ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
    ln_u(cnt[armIdx])
    ln_s(string(" of "))
    ln_u(ROUNDS)
    ln_c(" ")
    ln_s(arm_class(armIdx))
    ln_s(string("  settle "))
    ln_u(stl_lim(armIdx, armIdx, FALSE))
    ln_s(string(".."))
    ln_u(stl_lim(armIdx, armIdx, TRUE))
    nBj := 0
    repeat roundIdx from 0 to ROUNDS - 1
      if base_j(roundIdx, armIdx)
        nBj++
    ln_s(string("  baseline J "))
    ln_u(nBj)
    ln_c("/")
    ln_u(ROUNDS)
    if is_control(armIdx)
      if j_path(armIdx) == J_PATH_LS
        ln_s(string(" (judged, gate JL)"))
      else
        ln_s(string(" (judged, gate JB)"))
    else
      ln_s(string(" (printed only)"))
    if is_packet_arm(armIdx) and cnt[armIdx] == 0
      ln_s(string("  (dur in the OTHER speed's window in "))
      ln_u(count_other(armIdx))
      ln_s(string(" of "))
      ln_u(ROUNDS)
      ln_s(string(" rounds)"))
    ln_out()
  say(string(" "))
  say(string("--- GATES ---"))
  nJw := count_jw()
  ln_s(string("  (observation, not a gate) DP IN not high within "))
  ln_u(JW_TIMEOUT_CLK)
  ln_s(string(" clocks before the J write (the J was written either way): "))
  ln_u(nJw)
  ln_s(string(" of "))
  ln_u(ROUNDS * NARMS)
  ln_s(string(" arm records ; each is marked 'jw timeout' on its raw line"))
  ln_out()
  nFsJs, nLsJs, nD2Js := count_js()
  nFsJb, nLsJb := count_jb()
  ln_s(string("  JS full-speed-start arms (CFS A1 B D1 N1 N2 X SCF SB): line J (bits 2..0 = %001) after the J drive, before the WXPIN site, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(nFsJs == 0))
  ln_out()
  if nFsJs <> 0
    say(string("RIG FAIL: gate JS -- a full-speed-start arm's line was not J before its WXPIN site -- the arm did not start from the state the test requires"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  JB full-speed-start controls (CFS SCF N1 N2 X): baseline line J (bits 2..0 = %001) just before the packet, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(nFsJb == 0))
  ln_out()
  if nFsJb <> 0
    say(string("RIG FAIL: gate JB -- a full-speed-start control's baseline was not J -- the line did not hold the J its packet must start from"))
    fail_count++
  nLate := count_late()
  ln_s(string("  P  every write within "))
  ln_u(LATE_MARGIN)
  ln_s(string(" clocks of its deadline, all arms, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(nLate == 0))
  ln_out()
  if nLate <> 0
    say(string("RIG FAIL: a value was written late -- the packet's pacing was not the schedule, so no arm can be judged"))
    fail_count++
  nStl := count_settle()
  ln_s(string("  Q  settle in band, all arms, all rounds (short arms 0.."))
  ln_u(SHORT_MAX_CLK)
  ln_s(string(" clocks, the others "))
  ln_u(WX_CLK)
  ln_s(string(".."))
  ln_u(WX_CLK + SHORT_MAX_CLK)
  ln_s(string("): "))
  ln_s(yes_no(nStl == 0))
  ln_out()
  if nStl <> 0
    say(string("RIG FAIL: an arm's settle was not the settle the program states -- the short-settle arms were not short, or the long ones not long"))
    fail_count++
  nOvf := count_ovf()
  ln_s(string("  O  no event log overflow, all arms, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(nOvf == 0))
  ln_out()
  if nOvf <> 0
    say(string("RIG FAIL: an event log overflowed -- the decoded packet may be incomplete"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  K  arm CFS (no WXPIN, FS) GOOD in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(cnt[ARM_CFS] == ROUNDS))
  ln_out()
  if cnt[ARM_CFS] <> ROUNDS
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the control packet with no WXPIN step was not GOOD in every round -- the rig cannot carry a packet at full speed"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  N1 arm N1 (only 2 data bytes sent) returned exactly n=2 $A5 $5A in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(cnt[ARM_N1] == ROUNDS))
  ln_out()
  if cnt[ARM_N1] <> ROUNDS
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the short-packet negative control was not seen as a short packet in every round"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  N2 arm N2 (nothing sent) returned exactly n=0 in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(cnt[ARM_N2] == ROUNDS))
  ln_out()
  if cnt[ARM_N2] <> ROUNDS
    say(string("RIG FAIL: the nothing-sent negative control was not seen as an empty window in every round"))
    fail_count++
  ln_s(string("  X  arm X (FS pair, packet written at the LS schedule) NOT intact in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(cnt[ARM_X] == ROUNDS))
  ln_out()
  if cnt[ARM_X] <> ROUNDS
    say(string("RIG FAIL: a full-speed pair given the low-speed schedule returned an intact packet (or a dirty baseline) -- the speed is not what the rig thinks"))
    fail_count++
  bGateL := cnt[ARM_CLS] == ROUNDS
  ln_s(string("  L  arm CLS (no WXPIN, LS) GOOD in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(bGateL))
  ln_out()
  if bGateL == FALSE
    say(string("  (gate L does not hold: the low-speed arms A2, B, C, D1, SB, SC are NOT JUDGED; their records are printed above)"))
  bGateJl := nLsJs + nLsJb + nD2Js == 0
  ln_s(string("  JL low-speed J read J (bits 2..0 = %001): CLS A2 C SCL SC and D2 before the WXPIN site, CLS SCL at the baseline, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(bGateJl))
  ln_out()
  if bGateJl == FALSE
    say(string("  (gate JL does not hold: a J driven at low speed was not read as J -- the low-speed arms A2, B, C, D1, SB, SC are NOT JUDGED; their records are printed above)"))
  bLs := bGateL and bGateJl
  bGateJd := nD2Js == 0
  ln_s(string("  JD arm D2 (on D1's low-speed pair): line J before its WXPIN site, all rounds: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(bGateJd))
  ln_out()
  if bGateJd == FALSE
    say(string("  (gate JD does not hold: D2 did not start its WXPIN step from J -- D2 is NOT JUDGED; its records are printed above)"))
  bGateS := cnt[ARM_SCF] == ROUNDS and cnt[ARM_SCL] == ROUNDS
  ln_s(string("  S  arms SCF and SCL (no WXPIN, short settle) GOOD in every round: "))
  ln_s(yes_no(bGateS))
  ln_out()
  if bGateS == FALSE
    say(string("  (gate S does not hold: the short-settle arms SB, SC are NOT JUDGED; their records are printed above)"))
  say(string(" "))
  if fail_count <> 0
    say(string("NO VERDICT: a gating control failed (see RIG FAIL lines above); the records are printed for diagnosis only"))
  else
    nAll := 0
    nNone := 0
    nMixed := 0
    repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
      if judged(armIdx, bLs, bGateS, bGateJd) == FALSE
        next
      if cnt[armIdx] == ROUNDS
        nAll++
      elseif cnt[armIdx] == 0
        nNone++
      else
        nMixed++
    ln_s(string("  judged arms: ALL "))
    ln_u(nAll)
    ln_s(string("  NONE "))
    ln_u(nNone)
    ln_s(string("  MIXED "))
    ln_u(nMixed)
    ln_out()
    if nNone <> 0
      ln_s(string("VERDICT VO-J-035: DISTURBED - the packet after the WXPIN step was not GOOD in any round for:"))
      list_class(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd, 0)
      if nMixed <> 0
        ln_s(string(" ; and not GOOD in every round (MIXED) for:"))
        list_class(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd, 1)
      ln_settle()
      ln_out()
      if bLs and bGateS and bGateJd
        say(string("  while the no-WXPIN controls carried the packet GOOD in every round, a full-speed pair given the low-speed schedule did not, and the short / empty negative controls read short / empty"))
      else
        say(string("  control CFS carried the packet GOOD in every round, but a gate the other arms need did NOT hold: the arms that need it are not judged and are not listed above"))
      say_unjudged(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd)
      say(string("  scope: the baud set by the WXPIN, not D[14] (FS/LS signalling is not observable on this pair)"))
    elseif nMixed <> 0
      ln_s(string("VERDICT VO-J-035: INCONCLUSIVE - rounds differ for at least one judged arm (per-arm counts and raw records above); MIXED:"))
      list_class(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd, 1)
      ln_settle()
      ln_out()
      say_unjudged(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd)
    elseif bLs and bGateS and bGateJd
      ln_s(string("VERDICT VO-J-035: SURVIVES - after a WXPIN on the running pair (same word, FS to LS, LS to FS, FS-LS-FS; after a long and after a short settle) the baud set by the WXPIN was in force and the next packet was intact, in every round"))
      ln_settle()
      ln_out()
      say(string("  the no-WXPIN controls (FS and LS, long and short settle) carried the packet GOOD every round; the mismatched-schedule control X, the short and the empty negative controls read as not intact / short / empty"))
      say(string("  scope: the baud set by the WXPIN, not D[14] -- 'FS/LS mode changed' is NOT claimed (the FS/LS signalling bit is not observable on this pair)"))
    else
      ln_s(string("VERDICT VO-J-035: INCONCLUSIVE - every judged arm is GOOD in every round but a gate other arms need did not hold (L / JL / S / JD), so those arms are not judged"))
      ln_settle()
      ln_out()
      say_unjudged(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd)

PRI say_unjudged(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd)
' Print, under a verdict line, which arms are NOT JUDGED and why (nothing when every arm is judged).
'
' @param bLs - the low-speed half held (gate L and gate JL)
' @param bGateS - gate S held
' @param bGateJd - gate JD held

  if bLs and bGateS and bGateJd
    return
  ln_s(string("  NOT JUDGED in this verdict:"))
  if bLs == FALSE
    ln_s(string(" A2 B C D1 SB SC (gate L or JL)"))
  elseif bGateS == FALSE
    ln_s(string(" SB SC (gate S)"))
  if bGateJd == FALSE
    ln_s(string(" ; D2 (gate JD: its J before its WXPIN site was not J)"))
  ln_out()

PRI list_class(bLs, bGateS, bGateJd, wantMixed) | armIdx
' Append the names of the judged arms of class NONE (wantMixed 0) or MIXED (wantMixed 1) to the line being built.
'
' @param bLs - the low-speed half held (gate L and gate JL)
' @param bGateS - the short-settle controls held
' @param bGateJd - gate JD held
' @param wantMixed - 0 = list NONE arms, 1 = list MIXED arms

' Local Variables:
' @local armIdx - arm

  repeat armIdx from 0 to NARMS - 1
    if judged(armIdx, bLs, bGateS, bGateJd) == FALSE
      next
    if wantMixed == 0 and cnt[armIdx] == 0
      ln_c(" ")
      ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
    if wantMixed <> 0 and cnt[armIdx] <> 0 and cnt[armIdx] <> ROUNDS
      ln_c(" ")
      ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))

PRI print_arm(roundIdx, armIdx) | evIdx, nUse
' Print one arm's record in one round as a line; an arm that missed its required record also lists its event log.
'
' @param roundIdx - 0..ROUNDS-1
' @param armIdx - ARM_CFS..ARM_X

' Local Variables:
' @local evIdx - event index
' @local nUse - events listed

  ln_s(string("  r"))
  ln_u(roundIdx + 1)
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_s(arm_name(armIdx))
  ln_s(string(" base "))
  ln_h(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE))
  ln_line(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_BASE))
  ln_s(string(" n="))
  ln_u(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_N))
  ln_s(string(" bytes "))
  ln_h(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B0))
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_h(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B1))
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_h(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B2))
  ln_c(" ")
  ln_h(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_B3))
  ln_s(string(" sop "))
  ln_t(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_TSOP))
  ln_s(string(" tb1 "))
  ln_t(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_TB1))
  ln_s(string(" tb4 "))
  ln_t(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_TB4))
  ln_s(string(" dur "))
  ln_t(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_DUR))
  ln_s(string(" dwin "))
  ln_s(dur_window(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_DUR)))
  ln_s(string(" eop "))
  ln_t(dget(roundIdx, armIdx, D_TEOP))
  ln_s(string(" stl "))
  ln_t(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_STL))
  ln_s(string(" late "))
  ln_t(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LATE))
  ln_s(string(" w "))
  ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_W1))
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_W2))
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_W3))
  ln_c("/")
  ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_W4))
  ln_s(string(" ev "))
  ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_NLOG))
  ln_s(string(" last "))
  ln_h(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LAST))
  ln_s(string(" jw "))
  if rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JW) == NO_TIME
    ln_s(string("timeout"))
  else
    ln_u(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JW))
  ln_s(string(" jst "))
  ln_h(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JST))
  ln_line(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_JST))
  ln_s(string(" seen SE0/K/J "))
  ln_bin(line_seen(roundIdx, armIdx), 3)
  ln_s(string(" req "))
  ln_s(yes_no(arm_ok(roundIdx, armIdx)))
  ln_out()
  if arm_ok(roundIdx, armIdx) == FALSE
    nUse := rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_NLOG)
    if nUse > LOGMAX
      nUse := LOGMAX
    evIdx := 0
    repeat while evIdx < nUse
      ln_s(string("      event "))
      ln_u(evIdx + 1)
      ln_s(string(": status "))
      ln_h(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LOG + evIdx * 2))
      ln_s(string(" at "))
      ln_t(rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_LOG + evIdx * 2 + 1) - rec(roundIdx, armIdx, A_T0))
      ln_out()
      evIdx++

{ MEASURING ENGINE -- runs in its own cog; the ONLY code that touches the pair.
  No debug() here (DEBUG_COGS excludes this cog anyway). PTRA = @mailbox on entry.
  respt_ walks the mailbox: per round, per arm in ARM_ order, A_BASE..A_LOG+2*LOGMAX-1 (ARMW longs).
  Every arm starts (usb_start) with the J drive (drive_j: bounded wait for the DP IN, WYPIN #3, settle, RQPIN) BEFORE its GETCT at
  the WXPIN site; D2 calls drive_j again before its own site.
  The packet routine reads NO IN flag.  One loop does everything: it samples the status of DM (RQPIN, 18 clocks per plain
  pass), logs every change of bits 4..15 with the CT and ORs every sample it does not log into seen_; when CT1 (set with
  ADDCT1 to the next write deadline) has passed (POLLCT1) it writes the next value at once.  Value 0 (SOP) is written at the
  start; value k >= 1 is due at (SOP write instant) + g0_ + (k-1) * step_.  After the last write CT1 is set to the end of the
  observation window.
}
DAT             org     0        ' ---- Measuring Engine ----
engine          mov     mb_, ptra
                mov     respt_, mb_
                add     respt_, #MB_RES * 4
                mov     progp_, mb_
                add     progp_, #MB_PROG * 4
                mov     rnd_, #ROUNDS
                mov     roundno_, #0

.round          add     roundno_, #1
                wrlong  roundno_, progp_

                ' Every arm takes GETCT twx_ at the "WXPIN site" (just before the WXPIN, or where it would be): A_STL = SOP write - twx_.
                ' The J drive (inside usb_start; D2: call #drive_j) runs BEFORE that GETCT, so it is not part of any stl.
                ' LONG arms go through pkt4 / pktgen (WX_CLK wait); SHORT arms through pkt4s (no wait).

                ' ---- CFS: control, start FS, no WXPIN step, FS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- CLS: control, start LS, no WXPIN step, LS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_ls_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- A1: start FS, WXPIN FS (same word), FS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_fs_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- A2: start LS, WXPIN LS (same word), LS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_ls_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_ls_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- B: start FS, WXPIN LS, LS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_ls_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- C: start LS, WXPIN FS, FS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_ls_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_fs_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- D1 / D2: start FS, WXPIN LS, packet at LS (D1), J again, WXPIN FS, packet at FS (D2) ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_ls_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4
                call    #drive_j                ' D2: the J again (D1's packet ended in the automatic EOP, then IDLE, :4603-:4604)
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_fs_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- N1: negative control, start FS, no WXPIN, SOP + 2 data bytes only ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_fs
                mov     nvals_, #NSHORT + 1
                call    #pktgen

                ' ---- N2: negative control, start FS, no WXPIN, nothing sent ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_fs
                mov     nvals_, #0
                call    #pktgen

                ' ---- X: negative control, start FS, no WXPIN, the packet written at the LS schedule ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4

                ' ---- SCF: control of SB / SC, start FS, no WXPIN, FS schedule, SHORT settle ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4s

                ' ---- SCL: control of SB / SC, start LS, no WXPIN, LS schedule, SHORT settle ----
                mov     xs_, x_ls_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4s

                ' ---- SB: start FS, WXPIN LS, LS schedule, SHORT settle ----
                mov     xs_, x_fs_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_ls_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_ls
                call    #pkt4s

                ' ---- SC: start LS, WXPIN FS, FS schedule, SHORT settle ----
                mov     xs_, x_ls_
                call    #usb_start
                getct   twx_
                wxpin   x_fs_, #PIN_DM
                call    #sched_fs
                call    #pkt4s

                djnz    rnd_, #.round

                call    #usb_off                ' leave P0 / P1 safe
                wrlong  #MBOX_DONE, ptra        ' done -- always the last write
                cogid   self_id_
                cogstop self_id_

' All of it off: pair DIR low, smart pin off, OUT low.
usb_off         dirl    #PAIR
                wrpin   #0, #PAIR
                outl    #PAIR
                waitx   #100
                ret

' The documented start (silicon-doc-text.txt:4593): reset, WRPIN drive on both pins, WXPIN xs_ on DM, DIRH both;
' then the J drive (drive_j), so every arm reaches its WXPIN site with the line driven to J (judged by gate JS / JL / JD).
usb_start       call    #usb_off
                dirl    #PAIR
                wrpin   cfg_drive_, #PAIR
                wxpin   xs_, #PIN_DM
                dirh    #PAIR
                waitx   ##START_CLK
                call    #drive_j
                ret

' Drive J and let it settle -- called BEFORE the GETCT at the WXPIN site, so it is outside the settle (stl) and the same in every arm.
' Wait, bounded by JW_TIMEOUT_CLK, for the DP IN (the output FIFO has room, silicon-doc-text.txt:4595 / :4608); record the clocks it
' took (A_JW; NO_TIME = the bound ran out -- an OBSERVATION, printed, not a gate); WYPIN #3 = output J (:4602), written whether or
' not the bound ran out; wait J_SETTLE_CLK (>= 7 bit periods at either speed, :4624-:4626); RQPIN the status word (A_JST, judged by
' gate JS on bits 2..0 = J in).  Also clears seen_ (the line states seen in the coming packet window) outside the timed path.
drive_j         mov     seen_, #0
                getct   jt0_
.jw             testp   #PIN_DP                 wc      ' C = DP IN: the output FIFO has room
        if_c    jmp     #.jgo
                getct   jw_
                sub     jw_, jt0_
                cmp     jw_, ##JW_TIMEOUT_CLK   wc      ' C = 1 while the wait is inside its bound
        if_c    jmp     #.jw
                mov     jw_, ##NO_TIME                  ' the bound ran out: A_JW = NO_TIME (printed observation)
                jmp     #.jdrv
.jgo            getct   jw_
                sub     jw_, jt0_                       ' clocks until the DP IN was seen high
.jdrv           wypin   #3, #PAIR                       ' output J (writes DM, acknowledges DP)
                waitx   ##J_SETTLE_CLK
                rqpin   jst_, #PIN_DM                   ' the status after the J settle (A_JST); RQPIN does not acknowledge
                ret

' The write schedule and the observation window of one speed.
sched_fs        mov     g0_, g0fs_
                mov     step_, stepfs_
                mov     obs_, obsfs_
                ret

sched_ls        mov     g0_, g0ls_
                mov     step_, stepls_
                mov     obs_, obsls_
                ret

' The full packet (SOP + 4 data bytes) after the baseline.  pkt4s = SHORT settle: straight to the baseline, no WX_CLK wait.
pkt4s           mov     nvals_, #NTX
                jmp     #baseline
pkt4            mov     nvals_, #NTX

' Baseline, then one packet.  Settle (pktgen: WX_CLK wait; pkt4s skips it), AKPIN both pins (clears any IN left by the WXPIN /
' WRPIN / WYPIN), RQPIN the status word of DM and record it (A_BASE), then the packet.  The packet reads no IN flag.
pktgen          waitx   ##WX_CLK
baseline        akpin   #PAIR
                rqpin   st_, #PIN_DM
                mov     val_, st_
                call    #put
                call    #packet
                ret

' One packet.  nvals_ values (SOP, then data bytes) are written on the deadline schedule; the status of DM is sampled
' continuously; every change of status bits 4..15 is logged with its full status word and CT, and every sample that is NOT
' logged is ORed into seen_ (A_SEEN); cog 0 ORs in the logged samples, so the line states seen cover every sample (gate O).
' The routine ends at the end of the observation window (CT1 = t0 + obs_), not on any status bit.  Writes the record fields
' after A_BASE (see the CON block).
packet          mov     prevm_, st_                     ' baseline status, bits 4..15: the reference for "a change"
                and     prevm_, mask_
                mov     txi_, #0
                mov     late_, #0
                mov     lgi_, #0
                mov     novf_, #0
                mov     w0_, #0
                mov     w1_, #0
                mov     w2_, #0
                mov     w3_, #0
                mov     w4_, #0
                getct   t0_
                mov     tsop_, t0_                      ' replaced by the SOP write instant when there is a SOP
                mov     dl_, t0_                        ' the SOP is due at once
                mov     tend_, t0_
                add     tend_, obs_
                cmp     txi_, nvals_            wc      ' C = 1 when there is anything to write
        if_c    addct1  dl_, #0                         ' CT1 = the SOP deadline
        if_nc   addct1  tend_, #0                       ' nothing to write: CT1 = the end of the window

.poll           rqpin   st_, #PIN_DM
                mov     tg_, st_
                and     tg_, mask_
                cmp     tg_, prevm_             wz      ' Z = 1 while bits 4..15 are unchanged
        if_nz   jmp     #.event
                or      seen_, st_                      ' a sample not logged: OR its line state into A_SEEN (one instruction)
.chkt           pollct1                         wc      ' C = 1 when CT1 has passed
        if_nc   jmp     #.poll
                cmp     txi_, nvals_            wc      ' C = 1: a value is due ; NC: the window is over
        if_nc   jmp     #.finish
                alts    txi_, #txdata_                  ' S of the next instruction = txdata_ + txi_
                mov     b_, 0-0                         ' the value, fetched BEFORE the write instant is taken
                getct   tw_                             ' the write instant, 2 clocks before the WYPIN executes
                wypin   b_, #PAIR                       ' writes DM (and acknowledges DP, not read)
                tjz     txi_, #.first
                mov     tl_, tw_
                sub     tl_, dl_                        ' lateness of this write (>= 0)
                cmps    late_, tl_              wc      ' C = 1 when this lateness is the larger
        if_c    mov     late_, tl_
                add     dl_, step_                      ' the next deadline
                jmp     #.wr
.first          mov     tsop_, tw_                      ' the SOP write instant: the origin of every deadline
                mov     dl_, tw_
                add     dl_, g0_
.wr             mov     ts_, tw_
                sub     ts_, tsop_
                altd    txi_, #w0_                      ' D of the next instruction = w0_ + txi_
                mov     0-0, ts_
                add     txi_, #1
                cmp     txi_, nvals_            wc      ' C = 1 while more values remain
        if_c    addct1  dl_, #0                         ' CT1 = the next deadline
        if_nc   addct1  tend_, #0                       ' all written: CT1 = the end of the window
                jmp     #.poll

.event          getct   te_
                mov     prevm_, tg_
                cmp     lgi_, #LOGMAX * 2       wc      ' C = 1 while the log has room
        if_c    altd    lgi_, #logbuf_
        if_c    mov     0-0, st_
        if_c    add     lgi_, #1
        if_c    altd    lgi_, #logbuf_
        if_c    mov     0-0, te_
        if_c    add     lgi_, #1
        if_nc   add     novf_, #1
                jmp     #.chkt                          ' (the logged status word carries this sample's line state; cog 0 ORs it in)

.finish         rqpin   st_, #PIN_DM                    ' the last status (A_LAST)
                mov     val_, tsop_
                call    #put                            ' A_T0
                mov     val_, late_
                call    #put                            ' A_LATE
                mov     val_, w1_
                call    #put                            ' A_W1
                mov     val_, w2_
                call    #put
                mov     val_, w3_
                call    #put
                mov     val_, w4_
                call    #put                            ' A_W4
                mov     val_, lgi_
                shr     val_, #1
                add     val_, novf_
                call    #put                            ' A_NLOG: events seen, kept or not
                mov     val_, st_
                call    #put                            ' A_LAST
                mov     val_, tsop_
                sub     val_, twx_
                call    #put                            ' A_STL: SOP write (N2: start of the routine) - GETCT at the WXPIN site
                mov     val_, jw_
                call    #put                            ' A_JW: clocks the DP IN took before the J write (NO_TIME = bound ran out)
                mov     val_, jst_
                call    #put                            ' A_JST: status after the J settle, before the WXPIN site
                mov     val_, seen_
                call    #put                            ' A_SEEN: OR of every sample not logged as an event, in the window
                setq    #LOGMAX * 2 - 1
                wrlong  logbuf_, respt_                 ' A_LOG: the whole log buffer
                add     respt_, #LOGMAX * 8
                waitx   ##SETTLE_CLK
                ret

put             wrlong  val_, respt_
                add     respt_, #4
                ret

cfg_drive_      long    CFG_DRIVE
x_fs_           long    X_FS
x_ls_           long    X_LS
g0fs_           long    G0_FS
stepfs_         long    STEP_FS
obsfs_          long    OBS_FS
g0ls_           long    G0_LS
stepls_         long    STEP_LS
obsls_          long    OBS_LS
mask_           long    $FFF0                           ' status bits 4..15 (not the line-state bits 0..3)
txdata_         long    $80, TX0, TX1, TX2, TX3         ' SOP, then the data bytes

mb_             res     1
respt_          res     1
progp_          res     1
rnd_            res     1
roundno_        res     1
xs_             res     1
g0_             res     1
step_           res     1
obs_            res     1
nvals_          res     1
st_             res     1
tg_             res     1
val_            res     1
txi_            res     1
prevm_          res     1
b_              res     1
t0_             res     1
tsop_           res     1
dl_             res     1
tend_           res     1
tw_             res     1
tl_             res     1
ts_             res     1
te_             res     1
late_           res     1
lgi_            res     1
novf_           res     1
w0_             res     1                       ' write instants rel. to the SOP write, consecutive: w0_ (SOP, 0), w1_..w4_
w1_             res     1
w2_             res     1
w3_             res     1
w4_             res     1
self_id_        res     1
twx_            res     1
jt0_            res     1                       ' CT at the start of the DP-IN wait (drive_j)
jw_             res     1                       ' A_JW
jst_            res     1                       ' A_JST
seen_           res     1                       ' A_SEEN
logbuf_         res     LOGMAX * 2

